WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.269 --> 00:00:16.510
Tim Ferriss: Hello, boys and girls, ladies
and germs.

00:00:16.510 --> 00:00:17.610
This is Tim Ferriss.

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:19.460
Welcome to another episode of The Tim Ferriss
Show.

00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:23.000
I'm going to keep my preamble short so we
can get to the meat of this conversation with

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:26.009
my guest today, Aryeh B. Bourkoff.

00:00:26.009 --> 00:00:30.590
He is the chairman and CEO of LionTree, an
independent investment and merchant bank,

00:00:30.590 --> 00:00:34.710
advising and investing in transformational
CEOs and the companies they lead.

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:38.579
Aryeh founded LionTree in 2012, during a time
of unprecedented disruption.

00:00:38.579 --> 00:00:42.719
And since then, LionTree has supported an
expanding number of industries as they capture

00:00:42.719 --> 00:00:45.030
opportunity in an evolving digital economy.

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:47.170
We're going to touch on a lot of those points.

00:00:47.170 --> 00:00:51.370
Aryeh is also the founder of Kindred Media,
a digital media and podcasting company powered

00:00:51.370 --> 00:00:55.059
by LionTree, and serves on the boards of Yahoo
and Carnegie Hall.

00:00:55.059 --> 00:01:00.039
Before founding LionTree, Aryeh spent 13 years
at UBS, closing his tenure as vice chairman

00:01:00.039 --> 00:01:02.559
and head of Americas Investment Banking.

00:01:02.559 --> 00:01:07.259
Before UBS, Aryeh was a high-yield research
analyst and ranked as the number one cable

00:01:07.259 --> 00:01:11.100
and satellite analyst by Institutional Investor
for seven consecutive years.

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:15.729
Aryeh is a graduate of the University of California
at San Diego and resides in New York City

00:01:15.729 --> 00:01:16.729
with his family.

00:01:16.729 --> 00:01:18.810
You can find LionTree at liontree.com.

00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:21.990
And on Instagram you can find a number of
accounts @aryehbourkoff.

00:01:21.990 --> 00:01:23.330
That's spelled A-R-Y-E-H B-O-U-R-K-O-F-F,
@liontree_llc and @kindredpodcast.

00:01:23.330 --> 00:01:24.600
Aryeh, welcome to the show.

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:29.030
Nice to see you.

00:01:29.030 --> 00:01:31.510
Aryeh Bourkoff: Thanks, Tim.

00:01:31.510 --> 00:01:32.790
Great to see you.

00:01:32.790 --> 00:01:34.270
Thanks for having me.

00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:35.750
Tim Ferriss: Of course.

00:01:35.750 --> 00:01:36.750
Of course.

00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:38.390
I've been looking forward to this.

00:01:38.390 --> 00:01:42.600
And I wanted to start with, actually, a mention
in your bio.

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:48.040
So, ranked as the number one cable and satellite
analyst by Institutional Investor for seven

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:50.030
consecutive years.

00:01:50.030 --> 00:01:52.579
What makes a good analyst?

00:01:52.579 --> 00:01:58.009
Or what made you a good analyst, and what
are common mistakes that analysts make?

00:01:58.009 --> 00:02:01.420
Aryeh Bourkoff: Well, it takes me back.

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:05.189
A career has different chapters to it.

00:02:05.189 --> 00:02:06.189
Right?

00:02:06.189 --> 00:02:12.280
I actually think those chapters should be
related to one another and foundational in

00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:13.400
nature.

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:18.020
But I don't like to hover in one place for
too often, and I also don't like to think

00:02:18.020 --> 00:02:20.570
about the zero-sum game.

00:02:20.570 --> 00:02:24.569
So things I'm doing today have no relationship
with things I did before.

00:02:24.569 --> 00:02:29.640
But I'm glad you brought up this analyst job,
because I think a lot of people forget about

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:30.800
that part of my life.

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:40.821
But it was so core in training and also in
the relationships and the understanding of

00:02:40.821 --> 00:02:50.220
how to be not only a good purveyor of companies,
but also of my own emotional stability, I

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:51.220
would say.

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:55.610
And so, the way I would say that is, "analyst"
is often thrown around, but it could be like

00:02:55.610 --> 00:03:01.211
a therapist, an analyst, but in this case
a research analyst, which I was, as someone

00:03:01.211 --> 00:03:06.480
that really looked at securities or stocks
or bonds in a particular industry.

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:12.920
So for me, the industries were always media,
cable, telecoms, technology, consumer-driven

00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:14.490
companies.

00:03:14.490 --> 00:03:21.010
And at first, I looked at those companies
from a bond perspective, junk bonds, high-yield

00:03:21.010 --> 00:03:27.250
bonds, and then equities, stocks.

00:03:27.250 --> 00:03:37.640
And covering those stocks in those same industries
allowed me to, ultimately, get to a goal of

00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:43.640
helping investors make money with those recommendations.

00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:50.730
Now, how you do that is understanding the
company, its fundamental picture and, ultimately,

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:57.189
where it's going in the future, because everything
today should be understood, and everything

00:03:57.189 --> 00:04:00.800
tomorrow should have a lot of uncertainty
around it.

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:06.049
So pattern recognition, how to predict the
future, is the first thing that you really

00:04:06.049 --> 00:04:12.069
are supposed to be learning as an analyst,
in order to help inform the masses of which

00:04:12.069 --> 00:04:13.910
investments to make.

00:04:13.910 --> 00:04:20.199
But then, the emotional stability part of
it comes into play, when you get out in front

00:04:20.199 --> 00:04:28.640
of everybody and say, with some boldness and
some prose and some analysis, "Here's my view.

00:04:28.640 --> 00:04:31.400
I think I'm right."

00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:32.870
More often than not, you're wrong.

00:04:32.870 --> 00:04:33.870
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:38.539
Aryeh Bourkoff: And it's like watching two
kids in a schoolyard, when you were growing

00:04:38.539 --> 00:04:41.180
up, getting into an argument.

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:47.810
And you watched one of them clearly lose that
argument and then count down until you saw

00:04:47.810 --> 00:04:52.900
that they recognized, that they would have
the confidence to admit that they lost the

00:04:52.900 --> 00:04:53.900
argument.

00:04:53.900 --> 00:04:54.900
And there's always a lag.

00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:56.180
It could be 10 seconds.

00:04:56.180 --> 00:04:58.240
It could be weeks, could be years.

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:01.740
And that's all about capitulation.

00:05:01.740 --> 00:05:06.020
And so the first thing I learned was when
I was wrong, and I thought that I would be

00:05:06.020 --> 00:05:11.500
right, to quickly recognize that I didn't
have anything that was that interesting or

00:05:11.500 --> 00:05:15.490
different to say in front of everybody.

00:05:15.490 --> 00:05:18.450
And by doing that, it was very embarrassing.

00:05:18.450 --> 00:05:22.150
And these could even be minor things.

00:05:22.150 --> 00:05:28.750
But it built so much credibility later that
when you are right, it counted so much more

00:05:28.750 --> 00:05:30.350
and for so much more.

00:05:30.350 --> 00:05:35.440
And then it built your own self-confidence
that you really did have something.

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:39.840
And then, if you're really lucky... and that's
where the rankings coming into play...

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:45.400
when you say something that's different than
the masses, and you have the confidence to

00:05:45.400 --> 00:05:52.090
say, going against the grain, to predict the
future, then the markets will go with you.

00:05:52.090 --> 00:05:54.460
The investors will go with you.

00:05:54.460 --> 00:05:57.600
People will be profitable based on your recommendation.

00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:01.860
People will make money based on what you say,
and you build a reputation.

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:03.550
And that was a process.

00:06:03.550 --> 00:06:07.010
Tim Ferriss: This raises a number of secondary
questions.

00:06:07.010 --> 00:06:11.740
Well, the first, just for clarification, when
you're talking about taking a public stance

00:06:11.740 --> 00:06:18.630
and promoting or publishing an opinion, what
form does that take?

00:06:18.630 --> 00:06:21.099
Is it a ranking system?

00:06:21.099 --> 00:06:26.020
Is it a general sort of binary buy or sell
recommendation?

00:06:26.020 --> 00:06:31.069
Just for folks who are listening, to have
a more discrete understanding of what that

00:06:31.069 --> 00:06:32.539
opinion looks like.

00:06:32.539 --> 00:06:33.741
What form does that take?

00:06:33.741 --> 00:06:40.840
Aryeh Bourkoff: Oh, it has to be a published
report that has to have an initiation report.

00:06:40.840 --> 00:06:42.800
It could be a report on the industry.

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:48.620
So it has to have some perspective, some thematic,
and within it, some specific company recommendation.

00:06:48.620 --> 00:06:53.610
So let's just say I want to write a report
on the movie studios.

00:06:53.610 --> 00:06:58.210
And then within that report I would say, "Here's
my view of the movie studios" and specifically

00:06:58.210 --> 00:07:06.340
my recommendation on Disney, and Warner Brothers,
and Viacom at the time, and Fox at the time.

00:07:06.340 --> 00:07:09.320
And then you said, "Within these, these are
my recommendations.

00:07:09.320 --> 00:07:13.710
Buy, hold, sell," and supportive documents.

00:07:13.710 --> 00:07:19.340
And then, because you knew that no one would
actually go and read a hundred pages, you'd

00:07:19.340 --> 00:07:25.090
have to summarize it in a perfectly synthesized,
four-bullet point cover page.

00:07:25.090 --> 00:07:29.460
And before any of that stuff happened, you
have to go through internal processes to defend

00:07:29.460 --> 00:07:34.889
your arguments, where people internally have
the right to say, "We don't really think that

00:07:34.889 --> 00:07:35.889
works."

00:07:35.889 --> 00:07:40.819
But more often than not, they would let you
go, and let the public be your ultimate judge

00:07:40.819 --> 00:07:46.569
and juror, because that public embarrassment
would be much, far more damaging to you, and

00:07:46.569 --> 00:07:49.990
far more self-correcting, than anything they
would say internally.

00:07:49.990 --> 00:07:53.150
But they have, obviously, institutions to
protect.

00:07:53.150 --> 00:08:00.710
But those documents were full of analysis
and thematics and context, both context of

00:08:00.710 --> 00:08:03.710
the here and now, and the past versus the
future.

00:08:03.710 --> 00:08:06.680
But the ultimate goal is predicting the future.

00:08:06.680 --> 00:08:10.819
If I said to you, Tim, today is, let's say
Monday.

00:08:10.819 --> 00:08:14.870
And by Friday, I was going to tell you, based
on a certain pattern recognition, what was

00:08:14.870 --> 00:08:21.270
going to happen to these stocks, based on
my analysis, or, maybe better, a year from

00:08:21.270 --> 00:08:26.159
now, and you believed me because of my credibility
and our relationship, and we had a track record

00:08:26.159 --> 00:08:32.649
together, and that conviction level was high,
then you may invest, just based on my word.

00:08:32.649 --> 00:08:35.969
But at the beginning, you'd say, "I need to
do my own research.

00:08:35.969 --> 00:08:38.240
I'm going to read your report.

00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:44.459
Then I'm going to check it with other people's
reports, my own conviction level."

00:08:44.459 --> 00:08:46.600
Maybe I'm a counter-indicator sometimes.

00:08:46.600 --> 00:08:47.980
But hopefully, you are the indicator.

00:08:47.980 --> 00:08:53.310
And then, obviously over time, if you're right,
more often than not, you just start investing

00:08:53.310 --> 00:08:54.310
for yourself.

00:08:54.310 --> 00:08:56.649
You may not give it to everybody else as a
service.

00:08:56.649 --> 00:08:58.910
That's the motion of a career, right?

00:08:58.910 --> 00:09:00.870
Tim Ferriss: That is your evolution.

00:09:00.870 --> 00:09:02.220
It's not always evolution.

00:09:02.220 --> 00:09:03.220
Right?

00:09:03.220 --> 00:09:11.209
Playing with your own chips, and taking that
responsibility to make direct bets in that

00:09:11.209 --> 00:09:14.300
way, is not a leap that everyone makes.

00:09:14.300 --> 00:09:19.060
And I want to take a step back for a second
just to read a fantastic headline.

00:09:19.060 --> 00:09:20.620
This is a piece from The Hollywood Reporter.

00:09:20.620 --> 00:09:24.410
So, "How Aryeh Bourkoff Became Media’s Hottest
Dealmaker."

00:09:24.410 --> 00:09:28.830
So some of the groundwork that we're laying
right now is going to lead into that.

00:09:28.830 --> 00:09:31.100
But I wanted to mention that because it is
an exceptional piece.

00:09:31.100 --> 00:09:34.750
I thought it was a very entertaining piece
also that people should check out.

00:09:34.750 --> 00:09:39.240
Let's rewind to Alan Ginsberg.

00:09:39.240 --> 00:09:40.760
Not the poet.

00:09:40.760 --> 00:09:42.160
Different Alan Ginsberg.

00:09:42.160 --> 00:09:47.470
Who is Alan Ginsberg, and what are some of
the lessons that you learned from him?

00:09:47.470 --> 00:09:49.990
Aryeh Bourkoff: Alan Ginsberg gave me a shot.

00:09:49.990 --> 00:09:59.130
And he was a mentor and the person that first
hired me into this thing called Wall Street

00:09:59.130 --> 00:10:00.410
in New York.

00:10:00.410 --> 00:10:08.410
And remember, I came from California, had
no reason to be anywhere near this industry

00:10:08.410 --> 00:10:14.300
or Wall Street, didn't know anything, really,
of substance, coming out of university, except

00:10:14.300 --> 00:10:17.950
what everyone else knows, which is how to
communicate, look for a job, maybe how to

00:10:17.950 --> 00:10:20.020
write a little bit.

00:10:20.020 --> 00:10:24.310
Other people maybe have much more vocational
expertise in engineering and medicine than

00:10:24.310 --> 00:10:25.310
I did.

00:10:25.310 --> 00:10:26.920
I was a generalist, coming out.

00:10:26.920 --> 00:10:33.850
So, coming to New York, didn't know a soul,
and basically put resumes under windshield

00:10:33.850 --> 00:10:37.619
wipers, so like, yeah, "Here's my flyer.

00:10:37.619 --> 00:10:39.370
I have an interest in being here.

00:10:39.370 --> 00:10:41.240
I'll hustle, and I'll earn it."

00:10:41.240 --> 00:10:44.630
And then you have to convince people to give
you a shot.

00:10:44.630 --> 00:10:46.880
And Alan Ginsberg gave me that first shot.

00:10:46.880 --> 00:10:52.899
And when I interviewed with Alan, who himself
was, and is...

00:10:52.899 --> 00:10:57.779
We're still in touch... a creative person
in finance, because Alan's background, even

00:10:57.779 --> 00:11:03.370
though he was a lawyer and a financier and
so on, he also wrote for Rolling Stone magazine.

00:11:03.370 --> 00:11:04.980
He liked writing.

00:11:04.980 --> 00:11:07.240
He liked the way the world worked.

00:11:07.240 --> 00:11:11.839
And he wasn't a traditional person you think
of as a Gordon Gekko, Wall Street type.

00:11:11.839 --> 00:11:16.000
And so maybe that's why we took an interest
in each other, because we talked about how

00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:18.682
the world worked, and he gave me a chance.

00:11:18.682 --> 00:11:23.480
And so Alan hired me, and he said, I remember
he said, "You're going to be the first person

00:11:23.480 --> 00:11:27.520
that works for us in the high-yield bond group
at Smith Barney."

00:11:27.520 --> 00:11:32.100
And Alan ran the Smith Barney research group,
having come from Bear Stearns before that.

00:11:32.100 --> 00:11:36.740
And I think before that, he was immediately
at Drexel Burnham in Los Angeles, the Michael

00:11:36.740 --> 00:11:39.130
Milken high-yield bond shop.

00:11:39.130 --> 00:11:40.130
And I said, "Okay, great.

00:11:40.130 --> 00:11:41.130
I'm going to work for you.

00:11:41.130 --> 00:11:42.130
What do I do?"

00:11:42.130 --> 00:11:45.450
And he said, "You're going to help me cover
the industries that I focus on."

00:11:45.450 --> 00:11:47.310
And I said, "Which of those industries?"

00:11:47.310 --> 00:11:49.510
And he said, "It's media.

00:11:49.510 --> 00:11:50.510
It's telecom.

00:11:50.510 --> 00:11:51.510
It's technology.

00:11:51.510 --> 00:11:52.510
It's food.

00:11:52.510 --> 00:11:53.510
And it's restaurants."

00:11:53.510 --> 00:11:57.990
And I said, "I have no idea what you just
said, but I like to eat and like to go out

00:11:57.990 --> 00:11:58.990
to eat in restaurants.

00:11:58.990 --> 00:12:00.300
So that sounds good."

00:12:00.300 --> 00:12:06.610
Eventually those fell away, and I kept going
with those media and communications and technology

00:12:06.610 --> 00:12:08.720
industries from day one.

00:12:08.720 --> 00:12:15.211
And then I just decided to get that competitive
streak to be better than the rest and to work

00:12:15.211 --> 00:12:16.211
at it.

00:12:16.211 --> 00:12:17.211
And I eventually got the bug.

00:12:17.211 --> 00:12:21.270
But I remember thinking to myself, watching
him in action, "I will never be able to be

00:12:21.270 --> 00:12:27.060
as good at communicating as he is or as good
at analyzing companies as he is."

00:12:27.060 --> 00:12:31.240
And you just kept going at it until you eventually
get the groove.

00:12:31.240 --> 00:12:35.720
Tim Ferriss: So, two questions related to
what you just shared.

00:12:35.720 --> 00:12:44.959
The first is, why go from California, backpack
in hand, to Wall Street and even make the

00:12:44.959 --> 00:12:45.959
attempt?

00:12:45.959 --> 00:12:49.040
What was the draw or the reasoning there?

00:12:49.040 --> 00:12:53.120
And then secondly, why do you think Alan gave
you a shot?

00:12:53.120 --> 00:12:54.149
Why would he say yes?

00:12:54.149 --> 00:12:57.840
Why would he take that ostensible risk?

00:12:57.840 --> 00:13:04.350
Aryeh Bourkoff: I was in California at the
University of California in San Diego, as

00:13:04.350 --> 00:13:17.740
you mentioned, and I knew, as I was graduating,
that I wanted to get into the center of finance.

00:13:17.740 --> 00:13:20.670
I didn't want to stay in the periphery.

00:13:20.670 --> 00:13:26.089
If you're going to go into an industry, go
to the heart of it.

00:13:26.089 --> 00:13:27.089
Don't plan around the edges.

00:13:27.089 --> 00:13:29.700
Tim Ferriss: Why the interest in finance,
also?

00:13:29.700 --> 00:13:30.700
Why finance at all?

00:13:30.700 --> 00:13:37.899
Aryeh Bourkoff: This is the way I described
it to my parents, who are academics and looked

00:13:37.899 --> 00:13:42.790
at finance, I could tell, in a disdainful
way, like, "That's not a way to live your

00:13:42.790 --> 00:13:43.790
life."

00:13:43.790 --> 00:13:44.790
Maybe a lot of your listeners think the same
way.

00:13:44.790 --> 00:13:48.790
And I thought to myself, "Okay, I have to
present this to them."

00:13:48.790 --> 00:13:57.639
And I remember going to travel to see my mother,
and I said, "I'm going to go into finance,

00:13:57.639 --> 00:14:05.250
and this is the way that I think it's a worthwhile
way to live your life," because they were

00:14:05.250 --> 00:14:07.709
academics and professors and so on.

00:14:07.709 --> 00:14:15.570
And I said, "Entrepreneurs, people have hopes
and dreams that sometimes take the shape and

00:14:15.570 --> 00:14:17.710
form of businesses.

00:14:17.710 --> 00:14:23.070
And those businesses need money, capital,
to see the light of day, to realize their

00:14:23.070 --> 00:14:24.070
dreams.

00:14:24.070 --> 00:14:25.720
And that's finance.

00:14:25.720 --> 00:14:32.509
And if I can play a role in helping those
individuals get capital and have their dreams

00:14:32.509 --> 00:14:35.070
realized, that is a worthwhile life to live."

00:14:35.070 --> 00:14:36.070
Sounds like a good pitch, right?

00:14:36.070 --> 00:14:37.440
But I don't know if I really believe-
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:14:37.440 --> 00:14:38.440
Sounds like a good pitch.

00:14:38.440 --> 00:14:43.449
Aryeh Bourkoff: Good pitch, but I mean, that
landed well, and I mean, I got the blessing.

00:14:43.449 --> 00:14:46.029
Plus I was rehearsing, probably practicing
my interviews.

00:14:46.029 --> 00:14:48.980
But that's really what I believed and believe
to this day.

00:14:48.980 --> 00:14:51.019
And that's kind of what makes it interesting.

00:14:51.019 --> 00:14:56.670
You see the energy in someone else, that you're
able to recast a problem as a solution that

00:14:56.670 --> 00:14:59.019
is based on someone's real idea.

00:14:59.019 --> 00:15:01.730
And it's not just about moving pieces around.

00:15:01.730 --> 00:15:03.329
It's about creating something new.

00:15:03.329 --> 00:15:04.970
And I think that that wasn't enough.

00:15:04.970 --> 00:15:11.180
It was enough for my parents, but it wasn't
enough in New York, because a lot of people

00:15:11.180 --> 00:15:12.300
have that pitch, I think.

00:15:12.300 --> 00:15:16.810
Almost like I remember when I was in the college,
I went to some of these parties, and you would

00:15:16.810 --> 00:15:20.440
walk in with your friends, and the vibe would
happen.

00:15:20.440 --> 00:15:23.040
And I remember walking in with one of my college
friends.

00:15:23.040 --> 00:15:26.490
And he couldn't get any women to pay attention
to him.

00:15:26.490 --> 00:15:29.699
I remember he said to me one day, he goes,
"I don't know.

00:15:29.699 --> 00:15:32.940
My mother always says I'm attractive."

00:15:32.940 --> 00:15:38.110
I remember thinking to myself, "I passed the
test with my mother about my pitch, but when

00:15:38.110 --> 00:15:40.910
I got to New York, it didn't really land as
well."

00:15:40.910 --> 00:15:45.540
It may have landed, but it was in competition
with a lot of other people.

00:15:45.540 --> 00:15:54.199
And so it took me about four months in New
York to get to Alan, because the day I moved

00:15:54.199 --> 00:15:57.210
to New York was because of a job interview.

00:15:57.210 --> 00:16:00.170
And I thought I hit the jackpot, one of one.

00:16:00.170 --> 00:16:06.800
The day I graduated was a Sunday, and I think
Monday or Tuesday, I had an interview set

00:16:06.800 --> 00:16:09.259
up with a guy named Paul Ting...

00:16:09.259 --> 00:16:10.680
I don't mind saying it now.

00:16:10.680 --> 00:16:12.690
He's probably out there somewhere at Oppenheimer...

00:16:12.690 --> 00:16:15.990
And he covered the oil and gas industry.

00:16:15.990 --> 00:16:17.550
The oil and gas industry.

00:16:17.550 --> 00:16:20.269
I told myself I wasn't going to choose the
industry.

00:16:20.269 --> 00:16:24.430
Whoever hired me first, I would just be loyal
to that industry for the rest of my life —

00:16:24.430 --> 00:16:25.870
Tim Ferriss: Wow.

00:16:25.870 --> 00:16:26.884
Aryeh Bourkoff: — and I could build a specialty.

00:16:26.884 --> 00:16:34.360
And so I went and, incredibly, I got this
interview, and I had a meeting like this day,

00:16:34.360 --> 00:16:37.339
day two in New York, downtown, the big city.

00:16:37.339 --> 00:16:38.990
It was 1995.

00:16:38.990 --> 00:16:40.640
Yeah, 1995.

00:16:40.640 --> 00:16:45.660
And I thought the interview was great, and
I was going to be a research analyst working

00:16:45.660 --> 00:16:49.029
for Paul Ting, oil and gas, Oppenheimer.

00:16:49.029 --> 00:16:53.959
And at the end, I was starting to strut out
of the office and said, "Okay.

00:16:53.959 --> 00:16:54.959
Next step?

00:16:54.959 --> 00:16:55.959
When do I start?"

00:16:55.959 --> 00:16:58.690
This is like the same interview I had with
my mother.

00:16:58.690 --> 00:16:59.690
It's landing well.

00:16:59.690 --> 00:17:01.329
And he goes, "By the way, one more question."

00:17:01.329 --> 00:17:05.860
He goes, "Do you know the programming language
Visual Basic?"

00:17:05.860 --> 00:17:08.860
Like, "C+, Visual Basic."

00:17:08.860 --> 00:17:11.189
And I said, "No."

00:17:11.189 --> 00:17:15.699
And he said, "Well, I kind of need you to
know that if you're going to be on the team."

00:17:15.699 --> 00:17:16.920
These days, they call it a doorknob negotiation.

00:17:16.920 --> 00:17:21.250
On the way out, as you're holding the doorknob
and turn back and say, "By the way, one more

00:17:21.250 --> 00:17:22.250
thing."

00:17:22.250 --> 00:17:24.020
That's the whole negotiation, at the doorknob.

00:17:24.020 --> 00:17:25.310
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:17:25.310 --> 00:17:26.310
Right.

00:17:26.310 --> 00:17:28.750
Aryeh Bourkoff: So, that's what he said.

00:17:28.750 --> 00:17:30.280
"By the way, one more thing.

00:17:30.280 --> 00:17:31.280
Do you know Visual Basic?"

00:17:31.280 --> 00:17:32.280
I said, "No."

00:17:32.280 --> 00:17:35.340
And he said, "Well, you're going to need to
know that for me to hire you."

00:17:35.340 --> 00:17:39.270
I said, "Well, what if I learn it and master
it?

00:17:39.270 --> 00:17:41.300
Will then you hire me?"

00:17:41.300 --> 00:17:42.870
He goes, "Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:17:42.870 --> 00:17:43.870
Then we're good."

00:17:43.870 --> 00:17:45.059
That's New York City.

00:17:45.059 --> 00:17:46.250
I was like, "Great."

00:17:46.250 --> 00:17:52.090
I left, bought Visual Basic for Dummies, bought
a laptop, drained my account, and got subletted

00:17:52.090 --> 00:17:53.900
an apartment, started learning the thing.

00:17:53.900 --> 00:17:55.809
Two weeks later, I felt comfortable.

00:17:55.809 --> 00:17:58.020
"This is the world as it should be."

00:17:58.020 --> 00:17:59.020
Called them.

00:17:59.020 --> 00:18:02.200
And I never got a return phone call.

00:18:02.200 --> 00:18:03.290
Tim Ferriss: Oh, man.

00:18:03.290 --> 00:18:05.790
Aryeh Bourkoff: And I was like, "Well, what
do I do now?

00:18:05.790 --> 00:18:06.790
What I do now?"

00:18:06.790 --> 00:18:12.650
By the way, while I was at UBS in a senior
position in research, I did see across the

00:18:12.650 --> 00:18:19.409
tape, probably, I don't know, 15 years later,
that we hired a guy named Paul Ting at Oppenheimer

00:18:19.409 --> 00:18:24.650
to do oil and gas for UBS, when I was there
doing media.

00:18:24.650 --> 00:18:27.350
And I waited till he started, and I went down
the hallway.

00:18:27.350 --> 00:18:28.400
I knocked on door.

00:18:28.400 --> 00:18:31.169
I said, "By the way, Paul, you may or may
not remember me.

00:18:31.169 --> 00:18:33.340
I want to welcome you to UBS."

00:18:33.340 --> 00:18:39.760
At this point, I was an established senior
person doing media stocks, really well-established

00:18:39.760 --> 00:18:40.760
at UBS.

00:18:40.760 --> 00:18:42.300
And I said, "I just want to welcome you.

00:18:42.300 --> 00:18:46.549
And I want to tell you that I mastered that
fucking Visual Basic programming language

00:18:46.549 --> 00:18:51.110
and I wish you luck here, buddy."

00:18:51.110 --> 00:18:57.750
You're really putting on the other side one
of your philosophies in life.

00:18:57.750 --> 00:19:02.610
"The toe you step on today could be connected
to the ass you're going to kiss tomorrow."

00:19:02.610 --> 00:19:03.860
File away.

00:19:03.860 --> 00:19:06.970
And I still know him.

00:19:06.970 --> 00:19:09.460
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, good.

00:19:09.460 --> 00:19:10.460
Good mantra.

00:19:10.460 --> 00:19:11.710
Aryeh Bourkoff: So yeah.

00:19:11.710 --> 00:19:18.179
Four months later, left in New York, I became
a professional job seeker.

00:19:18.179 --> 00:19:25.669
And I would basically walk to different buildings
that looked like hitter buildings.

00:19:25.669 --> 00:19:30.300
And I looked at the directory at the basement,
at the lobby level, looked for what looked

00:19:30.300 --> 00:19:32.690
like Wall Street firms, the ones I recognized,
obviously.

00:19:32.690 --> 00:19:34.049
And then others I didn't recognize.

00:19:34.049 --> 00:19:35.049
And I would go up.

00:19:35.049 --> 00:19:36.049
This is pre-9/11.

00:19:36.049 --> 00:19:37.809
They didn't check IDs.

00:19:37.809 --> 00:19:41.370
I would go up to the HR floor, and I would
hand in my resume.

00:19:41.370 --> 00:19:45.970
And I would choose a different location in
Manhattan every week to tackle.

00:19:45.970 --> 00:19:48.470
The downtown, the midtown.

00:19:48.470 --> 00:19:51.110
And then one day, I stumbled upon Smith Barney.

00:19:51.110 --> 00:19:56.350
It was still on Greenwich Street downtown,
right across from the Greenwich Hotel.

00:19:56.350 --> 00:20:00.000
This is now an amazing, hot area.

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:03.740
Back then, it was away from Wall Street and
away from the action.

00:20:03.740 --> 00:20:08.559
And Karine Krenik, to her credit, since I'm
naming names, was the HR person that let me

00:20:08.559 --> 00:20:13.990
through the door and gave me a shot, wherever
she is, and introduced me to Alan Ginsberg.

00:20:13.990 --> 00:20:21.159
And by that point, I was pretty good on the
pitch and also self-corrective, knowing what

00:20:21.159 --> 00:20:22.990
didn't land and what landed.

00:20:22.990 --> 00:20:25.540
And Alan gave me a shot.

00:20:25.540 --> 00:20:27.659
And I think he liked the hustle.

00:20:27.659 --> 00:20:32.799
He liked the hustle, liked the background
probably, and also a little bit of luck.

00:20:32.799 --> 00:20:36.159
But he also called me out for bullshit.

00:20:36.159 --> 00:20:40.929
The proverbial question in interview is, "What's
your weakness?"

00:20:40.929 --> 00:20:42.820
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:20:42.820 --> 00:20:47.870
Aryeh Bourkoff: Remember, that's when I was
practiced.

00:20:47.870 --> 00:20:50.429
So I said, "My penmanship."

00:20:50.429 --> 00:20:53.360
And I thought that was pretty clever.

00:20:53.360 --> 00:20:57.870
And he said, "That's a bullshit answer."

00:20:57.870 --> 00:21:01.030
He went, "Try again."

00:21:01.030 --> 00:21:03.400
So then I had to figure that out.

00:21:03.400 --> 00:21:04.400
I stumbled through it.

00:21:04.400 --> 00:21:05.400
But we both chuckled.

00:21:05.400 --> 00:21:10.130
And I forgot what I said exactly, but I'm
sure I said something that was more sensitive.

00:21:10.130 --> 00:21:12.570
But he called me out on it.

00:21:12.570 --> 00:21:13.990
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:21:13.990 --> 00:21:14.990
Amazing.

00:21:14.990 --> 00:21:20.320
Well, it seems like perhaps a blessing in
disguise, life saving you from what you wanted

00:21:20.320 --> 00:21:22.539
with Paul and the oil and gas.

00:21:22.539 --> 00:21:25.350
But who's to say, I suppose, in retrospect?

00:21:25.350 --> 00:21:31.610
I want to take a closer look at Alan teaching
you how the world worked.

00:21:31.610 --> 00:21:41.520
And I'm looking at a Kindred Media piece on
Medium, and there's a mention of Alan.

00:21:41.520 --> 00:21:47.780
And I'll just read part of it, which is, "One
day, Ginsburg asked him..." (that's asking

00:21:47.780 --> 00:21:48.920
you) "...if he read the paper.

00:21:48.920 --> 00:21:50.590
'Thinking, "What deal did I miss?

00:21:50.590 --> 00:21:51.590
What happened?

00:21:51.590 --> 00:21:52.620
Did I miss a number?"'

00:21:52.620 --> 00:21:53.620
Aryeh reminisced.

00:21:53.620 --> 00:21:56.480
'[Ginsburg] goes, "No, no, no, no.

00:21:56.480 --> 00:21:57.480
Forget the numbers.

00:21:57.480 --> 00:21:59.090
The Opera House burned down in Florence.

00:21:59.090 --> 00:22:01.200
You didn’t read that?"

00:22:01.200 --> 00:22:03.520
And I said, "I didn’t even think to read
that."

00:22:03.520 --> 00:22:06.110
And he said, "You’re going to have to be
a little more open-minded about this."'"

00:22:06.110 --> 00:22:12.900
And the reason I bring this up is that, observing
you in person, and also doing research in

00:22:12.900 --> 00:22:18.809
preparation for this conversation, you, more
than almost anyone I've ever met, tie together

00:22:18.809 --> 00:22:23.460
many, many, many, many disparate worlds.

00:22:23.460 --> 00:22:29.200
And you seem to have pattern recognition not
just in one sector, but across sectors.

00:22:29.200 --> 00:22:34.830
Was there any other world educating that comes
to mind, and that could be through direct

00:22:34.830 --> 00:22:40.590
teaching or osmosis, observation, that came
to you from Alan or other mentors?

00:22:40.590 --> 00:22:41.590
Aryeh Bourkoff: Yeah.

00:22:41.590 --> 00:22:52.230
I appreciate that you said that because it's
much easier in life to stay in a box and a

00:22:52.230 --> 00:22:56.570
blanket, a compartment of the whole ecosystem.

00:22:56.570 --> 00:23:02.710
And by the way, there are places for people
to be in those boxes and to thrive in those

00:23:02.710 --> 00:23:05.320
boxes.

00:23:05.320 --> 00:23:11.170
When you start to move outside of that box,
or in the corners of that box, or the edge

00:23:11.170 --> 00:23:19.390
of that box, or outside of that box, and draw
outside the line, so to speak, it is a beautiful

00:23:19.390 --> 00:23:20.390
place to live.

00:23:20.390 --> 00:23:27.601
And it is also a dangerous place to live because
it's full of unknowns and uncomfortable, maybe

00:23:27.601 --> 00:23:30.120
groundbreaking, things.

00:23:30.120 --> 00:23:37.929
It reminds me of another philosophical line
of, "Conventional thinking is typically right,

00:23:37.929 --> 00:23:39.090
but seldom profitable."

00:23:39.090 --> 00:23:41.300
Tim Ferriss: That's great.

00:23:41.300 --> 00:23:48.670
Aryeh Bourkoff: It feels good to think like
everybody else and to be moving with a herd,

00:23:48.670 --> 00:23:50.270
but you're not going to make a difference.

00:23:50.270 --> 00:23:56.120
You could be wrong, but you may never really
be right in an outsized way if you don't really

00:23:56.120 --> 00:23:57.620
stretch into the unknown.

00:23:57.620 --> 00:24:00.570
And some people are not comfortable with that.

00:24:00.570 --> 00:24:02.190
But where do I get that from?

00:24:02.190 --> 00:24:07.960
I mean, I get that from everybody, but it
really goes back to self-work.

00:24:07.960 --> 00:24:17.789
Ultimately, you have to have a strong core
to be able to do that, because if you just

00:24:17.789 --> 00:24:22.770
move with the wind outside the box, then you
don't really know who you are.

00:24:22.770 --> 00:24:28.540
Tim Ferriss: So, just to interrupt for a second,
so, by having clarity on that core or a solid

00:24:28.540 --> 00:24:30.320
core, is that...

00:24:30.320 --> 00:24:34.500
If you wouldn't mind, sorry to interrupt,
but just expanding that for a second, is that

00:24:34.500 --> 00:24:40.149
a core set of values, a clear idea of what
you will or will not do, so that you don't

00:24:40.149 --> 00:24:45.559
end up conforming to whatever winds happen
to be blowing at that moment?

00:24:45.559 --> 00:24:46.559
Aryeh Bourkoff: Yes.

00:24:46.559 --> 00:24:47.559
Yes.

00:24:47.559 --> 00:24:54.000
It reminds me of the scene in Raiders of the
Lost Ark, that last scene where the evil spirit

00:24:54.000 --> 00:25:00.540
comes into the warehouse, and Indiana Jones
and his compatriot or his partner, his girlfriend,

00:25:00.540 --> 00:25:01.540
whatever...

00:25:01.540 --> 00:25:06.940
At that point, they're all intermingled...
are hanging on to a foundational pole with

00:25:06.940 --> 00:25:08.779
their eyes closed.

00:25:08.779 --> 00:25:17.100
But yet this spirit comes in and completely
destroys everything else and kills everyone's

00:25:17.100 --> 00:25:18.100
faces.

00:25:18.100 --> 00:25:19.580
And it's a horror scene.

00:25:19.580 --> 00:25:20.580
Right?

00:25:20.580 --> 00:25:21.580
But they survive.

00:25:21.580 --> 00:25:22.580
Right?

00:25:22.580 --> 00:25:27.270
And they survive because of hanging on to
this pole, hanging on to each other, and keeping

00:25:27.270 --> 00:25:30.640
their eyes closed, and just hoping their way
through it.

00:25:30.640 --> 00:25:34.970
And obviously, the metaphor is they're good,
and everyone else is evil, and that spirit

00:25:34.970 --> 00:25:36.280
recognizes it.

00:25:36.280 --> 00:25:37.280
Right?

00:25:37.280 --> 00:25:40.130
But I think about that like, you have to hold
on to a few poles.

00:25:40.130 --> 00:25:42.200
You can't just be floating around.

00:25:42.200 --> 00:25:50.270
You have to have a few poles of foundational
strength that you believe in, that are making

00:25:50.270 --> 00:25:53.809
up your core.

00:25:53.809 --> 00:26:08.070
And those are tried and true, but at the same
time, that allows you to be curious, self-adjusting,

00:26:08.070 --> 00:26:14.730
self-corrective, migrational around other
areas, and being comfortable with many different

00:26:14.730 --> 00:26:21.510
types of people that are not like you at all
because you have that core, and that creates

00:26:21.510 --> 00:26:29.400
a very balanced and textured life and existence,
and a way to build pattern recognition and

00:26:29.400 --> 00:26:36.159
perspective that most people would be locked
in pursuing.

00:26:36.159 --> 00:26:38.530
And by the way, I'm blocked in my own way
too.

00:26:38.530 --> 00:26:40.780
Everyone has certain blocks.

00:26:40.780 --> 00:26:46.610
But the more of that exercise that you can
do the more of a perspective you can get of

00:26:46.610 --> 00:26:51.130
where the world's going and where it looks
like to be different cultures while you're

00:26:51.130 --> 00:26:54.210
living in your own foundational strength and
values.

00:26:54.210 --> 00:27:01.510
And I think to me, that all goes back to the
basic foundation of self-awareness, self-work,

00:27:01.510 --> 00:27:02.669
self-correction, self-growth.

00:27:02.669 --> 00:27:08.960
And once you have that, you're a member of
society that's valuable.

00:27:08.960 --> 00:27:15.130
And then you look for that in others and run
away from people that are ignorant or biased

00:27:15.130 --> 00:27:23.020
or morose or also maybe a nicer version of
that is just want to go with the flow everywhere

00:27:23.020 --> 00:27:27.320
and don't feel comfortable with some out of
the box thinking because it's threatening

00:27:27.320 --> 00:27:28.929
to those foundational values.

00:27:28.929 --> 00:27:35.270
A way to say that from a finance perspective
is when I was a research analyst, I wouldn't

00:27:35.270 --> 00:27:40.940
feel comfortable putting a buy recommendation
on a stock before I'd hit the send button

00:27:40.940 --> 00:27:46.750
until I both felt the conviction of the buy
and the rejection of the sell.

00:27:46.750 --> 00:27:52.610
So I had to live with the sell recommendation
inside of me first for a little while to see

00:27:52.610 --> 00:27:58.179
if it felt comfortable or uncomfortable and
let it in.

00:27:58.179 --> 00:28:01.330
Let the poison inside for a while.

00:28:01.330 --> 00:28:04.720
If that felt too comfortable for me, I couldn't
in good conscience go to the buy.

00:28:04.720 --> 00:28:09.530
So I had to have a buy and the rejection of
the sell because the buy to me could have

00:28:09.530 --> 00:28:12.540
been a passive recommendation, not an active
recommendation.

00:28:12.540 --> 00:28:17.990
So you have to ask yourself, are you comfortable
going into a controversial scenario or do

00:28:17.990 --> 00:28:19.940
you want to just live in the non-controversial?

00:28:19.940 --> 00:28:24.059
Tim Ferriss: One more question on this core.

00:28:24.059 --> 00:28:35.330
Is this a, let's just call it a general imperturbability
or a solidity that was more a felt sense for

00:28:35.330 --> 00:28:36.330
you?

00:28:36.330 --> 00:28:43.429
Or was it something that you, at that time,
codified in some way where you're like, "I

00:28:43.429 --> 00:28:48.960
prioritize these five things" or "I resolve
to follow these following values?"

00:28:48.960 --> 00:28:55.299
Was it codified or was it more of a felt sense
of stability and solidity for you at that

00:28:55.299 --> 00:28:56.299
time?

00:28:56.299 --> 00:28:57.299
Aryeh Bourkoff: Excellent question.

00:28:57.299 --> 00:28:58.299
Both, they're interrelated.

00:28:58.299 --> 00:29:10.059
So the first time I remember feeling that
sensibility was probably in grade school being

00:29:10.059 --> 00:29:14.250
popular kid with the friends, everything.

00:29:14.250 --> 00:29:21.309
Not an outcast with the group, but a little
bit different and feeling inside a little

00:29:21.309 --> 00:29:22.309
different.

00:29:22.309 --> 00:29:27.450
And then the question is, do you feel bad
about being different or does it start to

00:29:27.450 --> 00:29:30.750
empower you or both?

00:29:30.750 --> 00:29:35.740
And I, felt like, well, let's test it out.

00:29:35.740 --> 00:29:43.100
And I remember thinking, I am very happily
situated where I was in eighth grade, but

00:29:43.100 --> 00:29:48.120
let me leave that to go to a new ninth grade
school that was an unknown, away from all

00:29:48.120 --> 00:29:49.450
my friends.

00:29:49.450 --> 00:29:56.179
And let's see if friends were the thing that
made me feel great or the inside was the thing

00:29:56.179 --> 00:30:02.410
that I could apply to new friends and keep
that train going.

00:30:02.410 --> 00:30:10.059
And I chose to apply it to the unknown and
chose a brand new place and left behind what

00:30:10.059 --> 00:30:11.350
I had before.

00:30:11.350 --> 00:30:19.470
Now, I wouldn't do that with family or a very
close relationship, but I had felt very comfortable

00:30:19.470 --> 00:30:24.210
doing it with the environment I was in, to
get myself in a new environment and knowing

00:30:24.210 --> 00:30:32.690
that it's me that can adjust and apply my
skills to different areas and not the environment

00:30:32.690 --> 00:30:36.100
around me that defined who I was.

00:30:36.100 --> 00:30:40.799
I could be more proactive defining environments
versus having it defined for me.

00:30:40.799 --> 00:30:44.519
I remember that back then and it's happened
a few different times in my life, whether

00:30:44.519 --> 00:30:51.630
it's moving different places or feeling comfortable,
I always say I feel comfortable everywhere

00:30:51.630 --> 00:30:54.269
but belong nowhere.

00:30:54.269 --> 00:30:55.539
That's a geographic comment.

00:30:55.539 --> 00:30:57.630
And that's true by and large.

00:30:57.630 --> 00:31:04.899
But I also have done that in my careers where
I feel after a certain amount of time in one

00:31:04.899 --> 00:31:05.899
area, move.

00:31:05.899 --> 00:31:11.730
Take these strong parts of what it's applicable,
maybe the industry knowledge into new areas,

00:31:11.730 --> 00:31:14.020
but learn areas then I didn't know before.

00:31:14.020 --> 00:31:16.149
And see if you can reconcile those two things.

00:31:16.149 --> 00:31:21.110
And then that builds that core with the unknown
together.

00:31:21.110 --> 00:31:28.380
That's a life of curiosity and creativity,
but also life of foundation and strength and

00:31:28.380 --> 00:31:29.791
contribution on the other hand.

00:31:29.791 --> 00:31:34.360
And that I call that the gray area, like you
are comfortable in the gray area.

00:31:34.360 --> 00:31:36.370
And I feel like that's a competitive advantage
for me.

00:31:36.370 --> 00:31:42.000
I can hang out in that gray area longer than
most people in the world, very comfortably,

00:31:42.000 --> 00:31:45.720
until I see the optimal scenario to pass on.

00:31:45.720 --> 00:31:49.820
MIDROLL
Tim Ferriss: Let's talk about perhaps that

00:31:49.820 --> 00:31:53.610
gray area, and maybe misapplying that term
to what I'm going to ask.

00:31:53.610 --> 00:32:03.429
But this relates to a story you told me last
winter, and if you don't want to discuss this,

00:32:03.429 --> 00:32:07.789
I can imagine it's sensitive, but we can also
cut it afterwards.

00:32:07.789 --> 00:32:12.320
But I think it came about because of the title
of my first book, 4-Hour Workweek, and someone

00:32:12.320 --> 00:32:18.389
said, "Oh, you should talk to Aryeh because
that is probably not going to be a driving

00:32:18.389 --> 00:32:21.220
philosophy," or something along those lines.

00:32:21.220 --> 00:32:25.470
Although I do think we have some similarities
as well in terms of per hour output and thinking

00:32:25.470 --> 00:32:27.090
about various things.

00:32:27.090 --> 00:32:30.880
And you told me a story, I'm going to get
some of the details wrong, so I'd like you

00:32:30.880 --> 00:32:38.549
to correct it, but about at one point realizing
that many meetings were being declined because

00:32:38.549 --> 00:32:46.799
your calendar was full and then deciding to
take an entire year to block out every Wednesday,

00:32:46.799 --> 00:32:52.169
I can't remember, from six to 10:00 p.m. or
something, to take all of those overflow meetings

00:32:52.169 --> 00:32:54.419
that were being canceled.

00:32:54.419 --> 00:32:56.539
Could you retell that story if you wouldn't
mind?

00:32:56.539 --> 00:33:02.941
Aryeh Bourkoff: Sure, so it all starts with
the concept of rejecting constraints on one

00:33:02.941 --> 00:33:03.941
hand.

00:33:03.941 --> 00:33:08.940
On the other hand, problem solving, which
is probably one of these terms that are thrown

00:33:08.940 --> 00:33:11.240
around so many times, problem solving.

00:33:11.240 --> 00:33:12.240
I'm a problem solver.

00:33:12.240 --> 00:33:14.279
I run a company on solving problems.

00:33:14.279 --> 00:33:16.270
And there's probably like, but who actually
does that?

00:33:16.270 --> 00:33:20.450
I mean, put yourself in a situation where
you actually solve a problem that seems insurmountable.

00:33:20.450 --> 00:33:22.730
And so one of those problems were time.

00:33:22.730 --> 00:33:25.820
I don't have enough time.

00:33:25.820 --> 00:33:32.380
Time being the constraint, just tackle solving
that problem because everyone just talks about

00:33:32.380 --> 00:33:34.441
that all the time.

00:33:34.441 --> 00:33:42.090
And the other part of the story is the passive
or active conscious or subconscious deprioritization

00:33:42.090 --> 00:33:50.200
of people that are, let's say, not as important
to you as your kindness team wants them to

00:33:50.200 --> 00:33:51.200
be.

00:33:51.200 --> 00:33:55.179
But you're really not going to put them on
your calendar in the prime spots.

00:33:55.179 --> 00:33:56.789
So you deprioritize them.

00:33:56.789 --> 00:34:02.380
And even if you are prioritizing them in your
mind and your heart and your body and your

00:34:02.380 --> 00:34:04.830
soul, your office will deprioritize them for
you.

00:34:04.830 --> 00:34:07.769
And people have become an expert at these
things right.

00:34:07.769 --> 00:34:15.250
Now over time, you build a luxury in life
of being able to do that and be more selective

00:34:15.250 --> 00:34:20.570
because as you mature, you want to spend time
with only things that are essential.

00:34:20.570 --> 00:34:21.990
Maybe you're running out of time.

00:34:21.990 --> 00:34:27.609
But when you first start a company, and we
first started a company with relationships

00:34:27.609 --> 00:34:35.800
at its core and with reputation, that's really
important to define the company, the concept

00:34:35.800 --> 00:34:42.940
of rejecting people or those relationships
in any form, is out of the question.

00:34:42.940 --> 00:34:50.260
So what would happen at the beginning of the
firm some 10 years ago was, I recognize that

00:34:50.260 --> 00:34:51.619
I was a funnel.

00:34:51.619 --> 00:34:55.440
People would want to figure out what was going
on at one point in time.

00:34:55.440 --> 00:35:00.700
There are very few things in your life that
light up all your contacts at one time.

00:35:00.700 --> 00:35:08.850
Let's say your wedding, your funeral, God
forbid, maybe a big job movement, a big news

00:35:08.850 --> 00:35:09.850
item.

00:35:09.850 --> 00:35:14.079
Imagine, and all of a sudden your phone lights
up.

00:35:14.079 --> 00:35:16.040
But most of the time it just smooths out.

00:35:16.040 --> 00:35:17.940
Certain people won't care about different
things.

00:35:17.940 --> 00:35:23.519
When you start a company, it will light up
everything on your contacts, hopefully.

00:35:23.519 --> 00:35:26.690
Or if it doesn't, you have a bigger problem.

00:35:26.690 --> 00:35:31.530
And so this was a company starting, based
on all of the relationships in my contact

00:35:31.530 --> 00:35:36.185
base and a place to put them and to cultivate
them, to build a skyscraper.

00:35:36.185 --> 00:35:43.810
But at the beginning it was just me and the
relationships and an assistant.

00:35:43.810 --> 00:35:47.790
And so I started to get wind of the fact that
certain people couldn't get on my calendar,

00:35:47.790 --> 00:35:52.800
which is obviously natural because you can't
put every single one of your contacts on your

00:35:52.800 --> 00:35:58.099
calendar at the moment you announce a new
company, even if it's a few weeks out, people

00:35:58.099 --> 00:35:59.819
are going to say, "Sorry, I can't make it."

00:35:59.819 --> 00:36:02.880
But imagine how that feels for somebody else.

00:36:02.880 --> 00:36:07.750
I want to support this new LionTree venture
and what Aryeh is doing, but you can't see

00:36:07.750 --> 00:36:08.750
me.

00:36:08.750 --> 00:36:14.020
What a horrible way to start a company.

00:36:14.020 --> 00:36:15.020
I don't care who you are.

00:36:15.020 --> 00:36:21.980
So after a while of reading and hearing people
sending me notes or emails, I recognize that

00:36:21.980 --> 00:36:26.180
there are certain category of people that
they were, because obviously the office knew

00:36:26.180 --> 00:36:31.800
enough to give them credit to prioritize the
Tim Ferrisses of the world right away, or

00:36:31.800 --> 00:36:37.810
they maybe prioritize things that would be
immediately like dollars oriented or transactional,

00:36:37.810 --> 00:36:43.260
which is also not the philosophy of the company,
but they wouldn't prioritize things that would

00:36:43.260 --> 00:36:50.010
be career advice or people that wanted to
work with the company as a vendor or business

00:36:50.010 --> 00:36:54.770
partner, things like that, that they wouldn't
even know, but I would know.

00:36:54.770 --> 00:36:59.380
So I brought my assistant into the office
and I said, "So what is this thing we're rejecting

00:36:59.380 --> 00:37:00.380
people?"

00:37:00.380 --> 00:37:02.220
They said, "Well, you don't have time."

00:37:02.220 --> 00:37:05.680
I said, "Yeah, but you get paid to say yes.

00:37:05.680 --> 00:37:07.119
I can say no."

00:37:07.119 --> 00:37:11.960
That's the job of the assistant to figure
out a way to say yes.

00:37:11.960 --> 00:37:14.770
And they said, "But yes, but Aryeh, you don't
have the time on your calendar."

00:37:14.770 --> 00:37:17.509
And I said, "Well, that's the question.

00:37:17.509 --> 00:37:21.079
Let's figure out the problem solving of time
now.

00:37:21.079 --> 00:37:25.770
What if I created a whole extra day in the
week?

00:37:25.770 --> 00:37:31.940
Would that unlock the potential for filling
in all these second tier like requests, so

00:37:31.940 --> 00:37:32.940
to speak?"

00:37:32.940 --> 00:37:38.020
And they say "Yeah, hypothetically, if you
had an extra day, we probably have a lot more

00:37:38.020 --> 00:37:39.020
to work with."

00:37:39.020 --> 00:37:42.349
I said, "Okay, let me think about it.

00:37:42.349 --> 00:37:43.349
I got it.

00:37:43.349 --> 00:37:44.349
Wednesday nights.

00:37:44.349 --> 00:37:45.500
You were right.

00:37:45.500 --> 00:37:46.500
Wednesday nights.

00:37:46.500 --> 00:37:48.230
But the hours are different.

00:37:48.230 --> 00:37:50.310
From 10:00 p.m. till 2:00 a.m."

00:37:50.310 --> 00:37:51.700
Tim Ferriss: Oh, gosh.

00:37:51.700 --> 00:37:57.819
Aryeh Bourkoff: From year one of the company,
I will do office hours.

00:37:57.819 --> 00:37:59.790
But no internal bitch sessions.

00:37:59.790 --> 00:38:01.990
There's no suggestion box.

00:38:01.990 --> 00:38:04.700
This is an external session.

00:38:04.700 --> 00:38:08.349
If people really want it that bad, I'll be
here.

00:38:08.349 --> 00:38:10.020
No one else internally has to be here.

00:38:10.020 --> 00:38:12.579
Just leave me a little food, a little drink,
and let's see who wants to be here.

00:38:12.579 --> 00:38:16.760
I'll be here and then I'll start the next
day at the same time, seven in the morning,

00:38:16.760 --> 00:38:17.760
eight in the morning, whatever.

00:38:17.760 --> 00:38:21.230
I'll eat it on my end for year and I think
it'll unclog the situation.

00:38:21.230 --> 00:38:25.150
And we've created what you viewed as a constraint
of time.

00:38:25.150 --> 00:38:29.410
I created time by eating into my sleep hours.

00:38:29.410 --> 00:38:33.170
So your 4-Hour Workweek is really a four-hour
sleep week for me.

00:38:33.170 --> 00:38:36.369
But anyway, but it worked.

00:38:36.369 --> 00:38:41.140
People showed up for a year till two in the
morning and it was the most interesting, grateful

00:38:41.140 --> 00:38:42.140
conversations.

00:38:42.140 --> 00:38:44.140
And it's just New York City at its finest.

00:38:44.140 --> 00:38:50.660
In the office, you're talking about life and
career and that paid dividends.

00:38:50.660 --> 00:38:57.230
Not business dividends, but reputational benefits
and humanity benefits.

00:38:57.230 --> 00:38:58.230
Who are you?

00:38:58.230 --> 00:38:59.880
What do you want to be at the end of the day?

00:38:59.880 --> 00:39:00.880
And I'm happy we did it.

00:39:00.880 --> 00:39:01.880
I don't regret for a second.

00:39:01.880 --> 00:39:02.880
Now I don't do that now.

00:39:02.880 --> 00:39:09.470
I would love for someone else in the company
to do that now because I'm in motion still

00:39:09.470 --> 00:39:15.500
and I want to be more selective and deep with
relationships versus volume-oriented right

00:39:15.500 --> 00:39:16.830
now.

00:39:16.830 --> 00:39:21.329
And I almost want people to compete for the
depth of those relationships with me versus

00:39:21.329 --> 00:39:23.400
the masses.

00:39:23.400 --> 00:39:28.930
But that's what the firm is supposed to be
to understand what is selective versus, but

00:39:28.930 --> 00:39:31.970
the firm needed that at the beginning to really
understand what we're about.

00:39:31.970 --> 00:39:33.630
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, amazing story.

00:39:33.630 --> 00:39:34.630
Wow.

00:39:34.630 --> 00:39:35.650
10:00 p.m. to 2:00 a.m.

00:39:35.650 --> 00:39:37.110
So two questions.

00:39:37.110 --> 00:39:39.930
How did you choose 10:00 p.m. to 2:00 a.m.?

00:39:39.930 --> 00:39:47.119
Was that just to separate the casual inquiries
from the true believers?

00:39:47.119 --> 00:39:48.430
That's question one.

00:39:48.430 --> 00:39:50.070
How did you choose 10:00 p.m. to 2:00 a.m.?

00:39:50.070 --> 00:39:54.480
And then the second, you mentioned something
that I think probably caused a number of people

00:39:54.480 --> 00:40:00.010
to perk up because it would be the opposite
of what many would perhaps say.

00:40:00.010 --> 00:40:02.510
And that is the assistance job is to say yes.

00:40:02.510 --> 00:40:03.849
You can always say no.

00:40:03.849 --> 00:40:12.450
I think assistance are viewed by many folks
as a protective layer, maybe a flack jacket

00:40:12.450 --> 00:40:17.270
to deflect as much incoming as possible.

00:40:17.270 --> 00:40:23.250
So I'd love to hear you just explain why 10:00
p.m. to 2:00 a.m. and elaborate on the assistance

00:40:23.250 --> 00:40:25.000
job being to find a way to say yes.

00:40:25.000 --> 00:40:31.810
Aryeh Bourkoff: Well, 10:00 p.m. to 2:00 a.m.
is easy because I want to leave room for dinner.

00:40:31.810 --> 00:40:37.280
Not dinner, God forbid, like a personal dinner.

00:40:37.280 --> 00:40:38.370
A client dinner, a dinner outside.

00:40:38.370 --> 00:40:40.690
Business and life for me are very well integrated.

00:40:40.690 --> 00:40:47.259
So it would be used the city, go to dinner,
and then after dinner I come back.

00:40:47.259 --> 00:40:48.259
Some people still bring this up.

00:40:48.259 --> 00:40:52.040
People see me on the street, they say, "Aryeh,
are you still doing three dinners a night?"

00:40:52.040 --> 00:40:55.920
And I'm like, "That may have happened one
time, maybe 10 times."

00:40:55.920 --> 00:40:58.670
But then two times I would do dinner, I would
go to two dinners a night.

00:40:58.670 --> 00:41:05.020
I was just try to get it all in and not eat
as much at each dinner and move around.

00:41:05.020 --> 00:41:09.920
And probably in retrospect, was it the best
thing to convey to people.

00:41:09.920 --> 00:41:14.240
But except for the fact that I was working
hard and try to make sure everyone was happy

00:41:14.240 --> 00:41:15.530
and that I would get it all in.

00:41:15.530 --> 00:41:19.579
But at this 10:00 to 2:00 was about making
sure that that night I would have room for

00:41:19.579 --> 00:41:26.220
my dinner meeting or out of the office, using
New York, great city to do that.

00:41:26.220 --> 00:41:30.041
And then came back to the office, enough time
and then starting 10:00 p.m. and then I wanted

00:41:30.041 --> 00:41:31.359
to have enough time.

00:41:31.359 --> 00:41:39.960
There's this great song, I think it's You
Too song that has a line that says the magic

00:41:39.960 --> 00:41:48.040
hours are when it's not yet the morning, but
it's still night, but it's not really nighttime.

00:41:48.040 --> 00:41:49.190
And so you haven't yet figured out that it's
morning time.

00:41:49.190 --> 00:41:54.360
You haven't started thinking about your next
day yet, but it's past normal nighttime for

00:41:54.360 --> 00:41:55.360
everybody else.

00:41:55.360 --> 00:41:57.700
So you really can't be bothered.

00:41:57.700 --> 00:42:02.830
And none of us really live that way, I think
day to day.

00:42:02.830 --> 00:42:06.290
But that must be where a lot of magic happens.

00:42:06.290 --> 00:42:10.220
And then you get to a point where you start
thinking, Oh, my gosh, tomorrow morning my

00:42:10.220 --> 00:42:11.760
calendar is this and that.

00:42:11.760 --> 00:42:13.710
That, to me, starts around two in the morning.

00:42:13.710 --> 00:42:17.900
And so if I went past two, I'd start eating
into what I have to start doing the next day.

00:42:17.900 --> 00:42:18.970
Yeah, I'll remember the song.

00:42:18.970 --> 00:42:20.360
I'll remember the song.

00:42:20.360 --> 00:42:23.849
I just listened to the on the internet for
the first time again.

00:42:23.849 --> 00:42:24.849
Tim Ferriss: No problem.

00:42:24.849 --> 00:42:26.069
Could put in the show notes too.

00:42:26.069 --> 00:42:27.069
Aryeh Bourkoff: Yeah, yeah.

00:42:27.069 --> 00:42:28.359
That's a great song.

00:42:28.359 --> 00:42:29.720
Oh, yeah.

00:42:29.720 --> 00:42:39.569
"When the night has no end and the day yet
to begin, when the room spins around, I need

00:42:39.569 --> 00:42:40.569
your love."

00:42:40.569 --> 00:42:43.190
It's "Hawkmoon Rising," Rattle and Hum.

00:42:43.190 --> 00:42:49.760
YouTube on the Rattle and Hum album was the
only place you could find, I think.

00:42:49.760 --> 00:42:51.400
Rattle and Hum, YouTube, "Hawkmoon."

00:42:51.400 --> 00:42:52.700
Tim Ferriss: Good memory.

00:42:52.700 --> 00:42:54.309
Aryeh Bourkoff: There you go.

00:42:54.309 --> 00:42:56.690
Tim Ferriss: And the assistant.

00:42:56.690 --> 00:42:57.691
Aryeh Bourkoff: The assistant.

00:42:57.691 --> 00:43:04.070
The complexity of the job of an assistant,
particularly for me or for anyone in a relationship

00:43:04.070 --> 00:43:12.541
business where it's meaningful and there's
a long bill to it and a long tail to it, and

00:43:12.541 --> 00:43:20.780
genuinely is probably the most complex, respected
job in the company.

00:43:20.780 --> 00:43:28.510
And it is a puzzle piece that is, I always
think of it as those images of broadcast television

00:43:28.510 --> 00:43:34.320
when you're like, "Okay, I have Thursday night,
must see TV, and what's going to be the six

00:43:34.320 --> 00:43:35.320
o'clock hour?

00:43:35.320 --> 00:43:36.320
What's going to be the seven o'clock hour?

00:43:36.320 --> 00:43:37.329
What's going to be eight o'clock hour?

00:43:37.329 --> 00:43:38.660
And how's one going to lead into the other?

00:43:38.660 --> 00:43:39.859
And how's it going to fit in?

00:43:39.859 --> 00:43:43.060
Are people going to be still botched at 10
o'clock when it's over?

00:43:43.060 --> 00:43:46.520
And what's going to be the show after the
Super Bowl that you promote?"

00:43:46.520 --> 00:43:57.030
How the calendar flows and behaves is a living,
breathing animal with a soul and a brain and

00:43:57.030 --> 00:43:58.250
everything else.

00:43:58.250 --> 00:44:05.390
And the people that have to construct that
every single day, today and into the future.

00:44:05.390 --> 00:44:11.650
The assistant, I think is, I respect that
brain power and that knowledge more than anything,

00:44:11.650 --> 00:44:15.660
in some cases, including my role.

00:44:15.660 --> 00:44:24.660
So if you start with no, instead of yes, then
you've stopped playing the puzzle.

00:44:24.660 --> 00:44:28.040
You've said, I can't fit the puzzle piece,
so it's not going to fit somewhere.

00:44:28.040 --> 00:44:31.320
But if you say yes, then the puzzle keeps
expanding.

00:44:31.320 --> 00:44:37.480
And the calendar has to mimic the strategy
of the firm where I want to be going, the

00:44:37.480 --> 00:44:41.990
changes in the development of the firm, the
changes in the people I want to play with,

00:44:41.990 --> 00:44:46.569
work with, experience life with.

00:44:46.569 --> 00:44:50.390
And the understanding of that has to mimic
one another.

00:44:50.390 --> 00:44:53.450
And so it's not, they say yes, I say no.

00:44:53.450 --> 00:45:00.440
It is much more of we understand where we're
going and the calendar has to reflect the

00:45:00.440 --> 00:45:04.700
vision of what's going on in my head, and
therefore has to be communicated that way.

00:45:04.700 --> 00:45:06.920
And then it has to have a yes around it somewhere.

00:45:06.920 --> 00:45:09.040
Tim Ferriss: I dig it.

00:45:09.040 --> 00:45:10.040
Yeah.

00:45:10.040 --> 00:45:11.040
Thematically, makes sense.

00:45:11.040 --> 00:45:14.280
I want to hop to UBS here, and-
Aryeh Bourkoff: I'll tell you one more thing

00:45:14.280 --> 00:45:19.220
about the calendar, by the way, because I
talk about it a lot.

00:45:19.220 --> 00:45:25.609
If you boil it down, internal meetings, everyone
has a ratio.

00:45:25.609 --> 00:45:34.640
So if you did nothing because of your reputation
and your brand and your substance, your calendar

00:45:34.640 --> 00:45:35.710
is going to be full.

00:45:35.710 --> 00:45:40.050
People are going to want to get on your calendar
all the time, without you even choosing who

00:45:40.050 --> 00:45:42.560
you want to see.

00:45:42.560 --> 00:45:43.790
That's what I call defense.

00:45:43.790 --> 00:45:49.240
And if put that aside for a second and then
you had a blank sheet paper and said, "Forget

00:45:49.240 --> 00:45:50.240
that calendar.

00:45:50.240 --> 00:45:54.700
I, Tim, want to choose exactly who I want
to see."

00:45:54.700 --> 00:45:58.970
And that may not fill the whole calendar every
single day, but it'll be proactive, it'll

00:45:58.970 --> 00:45:59.970
be offense.

00:45:59.970 --> 00:46:00.970
So what's defense and what's offense?

00:46:00.970 --> 00:46:05.160
How everyone has to create a balance of those
two things in their calendar.

00:46:05.160 --> 00:46:08.040
Who wants to see them and who do you want
to see?

00:46:08.040 --> 00:46:09.380
And that mix.

00:46:09.380 --> 00:46:12.559
And then there's the internal versus the external.

00:46:12.559 --> 00:46:19.270
And there's no way around the fact that internal
meetings are cost or investment.

00:46:19.270 --> 00:46:23.809
Doesn't have to be negative, could be, but
they're necessary investments in the company

00:46:23.809 --> 00:46:31.109
or cost and external meetings should be revenue
or potentially for future revenue.

00:46:31.109 --> 00:46:33.040
So one's revenue, one's cost.

00:46:33.040 --> 00:46:41.430
If your calendar is 50/50, offense, defense
or external, internal or revenue, cost, you

00:46:41.430 --> 00:46:42.849
haven't done anything.

00:46:42.849 --> 00:46:44.280
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:46:44.280 --> 00:46:46.950
Aryeh Bourkoff: You're running in place.

00:46:46.950 --> 00:46:49.839
And so I'm very cognizant of, "What's the
ratio?

00:46:49.839 --> 00:46:50.869
What's the ratio?"

00:46:50.869 --> 00:46:55.880
And so I've figured out a lot time I go, I
really would love the ratio to be like 70/30

00:46:55.880 --> 00:46:59.030
external versus internal or maybe 80/20.

00:46:59.030 --> 00:47:03.270
And then you could start thinking to yourself,
"Okay, well that builds a good margin.

00:47:03.270 --> 00:47:06.760
And then you start, and then different times
of the year have different moments.

00:47:06.760 --> 00:47:10.420
Obviously if you're paying bonuses, you're
doing reviews, maybe there's a little more

00:47:10.420 --> 00:47:13.369
internal, but by and large, the internal should
be more predictable.

00:47:13.369 --> 00:47:17.070
If you're managing a good organization, you
could probably put the internal on your calendar

00:47:17.070 --> 00:47:18.640
for the entire year.

00:47:18.640 --> 00:47:21.040
Otherwise, there's surprises going on.

00:47:21.040 --> 00:47:24.270
And if there's more surprises going on, the
less well managed the company is.

00:47:24.270 --> 00:47:28.830
So you should be able to pretty much put the
internal on your calendar right now for the

00:47:28.830 --> 00:47:29.830
whole year.

00:47:29.830 --> 00:47:33.860
And then the external, you'd be surprised
at how much external you can put in your calendar

00:47:33.860 --> 00:47:34.860
in advance also.

00:47:34.860 --> 00:47:37.670
"I'll meet you at Formula 1 in Austin, Tim.

00:47:37.670 --> 00:47:39.240
Do you want to go again this year?"

00:47:39.240 --> 00:47:44.930
This is a date, or this conference or this
event or this holiday.

00:47:44.930 --> 00:47:48.480
And you could probably put the tent poles
of your external on the calendar.

00:47:48.480 --> 00:47:53.350
You'd be surprised how little room that leaves
for the spontaneity and the serendipity of

00:47:53.350 --> 00:47:55.050
life, which it must.

00:47:55.050 --> 00:47:58.900
So something has to then give absent sleeping
hours.

00:47:58.900 --> 00:48:00.720
And so that's what you're playing with.

00:48:00.720 --> 00:48:12.970
You're playing with these marginal slivers
of space that is growth, that is a choice,

00:48:12.970 --> 00:48:17.250
that is what's different today versus last
year.

00:48:17.250 --> 00:48:22.371
And that will represent who you are today
versus last year and what you want to be in

00:48:22.371 --> 00:48:24.050
the future.

00:48:24.050 --> 00:48:29.500
And whether the best companies in the world
is going to stay exactly where they were or

00:48:29.500 --> 00:48:30.500
evolve.

00:48:30.500 --> 00:48:35.270
There's no accident that most companies don't
evolve.

00:48:35.270 --> 00:48:37.800
You really have to work at it, a constant
build.

00:48:37.800 --> 00:48:41.460
You're dealing with slivers of space and time
to make that happen.

00:48:41.460 --> 00:48:43.810
Tim Ferriss: I'm glad you added that.

00:48:43.810 --> 00:48:49.359
I'm taking a number of notes for myself, so
that's why I'm scribbling furiously over here.

00:48:49.359 --> 00:48:51.109
You mentioned briefly-
Aryeh Bourkoff: I would cancel all internal

00:48:51.109 --> 00:48:52.690
meetings for you, by the way.

00:48:52.690 --> 00:48:55.640
To see you, I would take my internal meetings
off this.

00:48:55.640 --> 00:48:56.790
I would just, on a moment's notice, I would
go fly and see you.

00:48:56.790 --> 00:48:58.210
That would be like, that's the sign of true
respect.

00:48:58.210 --> 00:49:00.750
Tim Ferriss: Oh, well thank you for saying
that.

00:49:00.750 --> 00:49:05.450
And I look forward to the next time we're
able to spend some time in person.

00:49:05.450 --> 00:49:08.329
And I'm very much enjoying this entire conversation.

00:49:08.329 --> 00:49:11.250
I've been looking forward to this and bonuses.

00:49:11.250 --> 00:49:16.310
You mentioned bonuses, and I think this will
tie a number of threads together, some of

00:49:16.310 --> 00:49:20.690
which are yet to come, but we're skipping
ahead a bit.

00:49:20.690 --> 00:49:22.910
But you ran investment banking, or certainly
you ran investment banking in the Americas

00:49:22.910 --> 00:49:27.910
for UBS and had something like 1,500 people
reporting to you.

00:49:27.910 --> 00:49:33.090
When people came to you or bankers came to
you, talk to you about annual bonuses, as

00:49:33.090 --> 00:49:36.589
I understand it from the Hollywood Reporter
piece, you would ask, "What is your edge?"

00:49:36.589 --> 00:49:42.559
And I would love to hear you just elaborate
on the function of that question and what

00:49:42.559 --> 00:49:43.990
you mean by that.

00:49:43.990 --> 00:49:48.270
Aryeh Bourkoff: It goes back to how you're
judged.

00:49:48.270 --> 00:49:51.319
Ideally, you're judging yourself every single
day.

00:49:51.319 --> 00:49:55.140
Everyone's making their own adjustments every
single day.

00:49:55.140 --> 00:50:02.220
And then you have external factors which remind
you how you're doing, every single day.

00:50:02.220 --> 00:50:08.369
And there's a lot of haphazard activity, and
not everything's logic, based on logic and

00:50:08.369 --> 00:50:09.369
reason.

00:50:09.369 --> 00:50:14.819
But if you're running a business, you'd have
a daily profit and loss statement, which tells

00:50:14.819 --> 00:50:15.819
you how you're doing.

00:50:15.819 --> 00:50:21.490
If you own a restaurant, tells you what you're
doing, if you run a investment fund, tells

00:50:21.490 --> 00:50:31.040
what you're doing, that all those things contribute
to your own humility and self correctness.

00:50:31.040 --> 00:50:39.900
If you're paying somebody a bonus or a compensation
at the end of the year, you are the one that's

00:50:39.900 --> 00:50:44.930
creating that self-awareness in that person.

00:50:44.930 --> 00:50:55.869
I've never seen anybody come into a conversation
around compensation and feel as an employee

00:50:55.869 --> 00:51:02.790
that their boss has synced up exactly with
what they think that they're worth.

00:51:02.790 --> 00:51:04.640
I've never seen it.

00:51:04.640 --> 00:51:13.000
So within that, there's a chasm of misunderstanding
or emotion of what you think you're worth

00:51:13.000 --> 00:51:17.580
versus what they think you're worth, on a
given year.

00:51:17.580 --> 00:51:18.580
Okay.

00:51:18.580 --> 00:51:19.680
And that's normal life.

00:51:19.680 --> 00:51:25.770
Ideally, you'd like to have some daily metric
that just defines that, that daily P and L.

00:51:25.770 --> 00:51:28.390
So at the end of the year, there's no one
big event.

00:51:28.390 --> 00:51:37.309
So what I was trying to do is to try to build
in as much of that narrowing of that chasm

00:51:37.309 --> 00:51:44.430
as possible before the conversation because
it is a negotiating strategy, or you could

00:51:44.430 --> 00:51:46.710
call it a real effort to just build awareness,
saying...

00:51:46.710 --> 00:51:54.440
The best way to do that is to ask someone
to define who they are, you'd be surprised

00:51:54.440 --> 00:52:02.560
when you'd ask them to talk about themselves
how passive they will be and either how little

00:52:02.560 --> 00:52:09.210
they will value themselves in the right way,
and how much they over-value themselves in

00:52:09.210 --> 00:52:10.210
the wrong way.

00:52:10.210 --> 00:52:13.530
So I feel like people always say, "What is
your superpower?"

00:52:13.530 --> 00:52:16.930
and maybe those things are thrown around too
often these days.

00:52:16.930 --> 00:52:18.300
What's your competitive advantage?

00:52:18.300 --> 00:52:20.150
What's your edge?

00:52:20.150 --> 00:52:27.570
In a certain defined role like the role of
finance or the role of banking, I said, "The

00:52:27.570 --> 00:52:36.200
edge really comes into play of do you have
a unique idea, do you bring unique capital,

00:52:36.200 --> 00:52:39.450
or do you have a unique relationship?"

00:52:39.450 --> 00:52:40.760
But I wouldn't give them the answer.

00:52:40.760 --> 00:52:43.119
I was trying to figure out if they could get
to it.

00:52:43.119 --> 00:52:46.430
If someone says, "I work harder than everybody
else," okay.

00:52:46.430 --> 00:52:52.099
Imagine telling an NFL player or NBA player,
"I work harder."

00:52:52.099 --> 00:52:54.130
Is that really an edge these days?

00:52:54.130 --> 00:52:59.420
I mean, one of these we hear like, "I'm doing
the best I can," I'm like, "Okay.

00:52:59.420 --> 00:53:02.920
Well, then you get out of bed, and then what
happens?"

00:53:02.920 --> 00:53:06.220
I'm assuming everyone's doing the best they
can.

00:53:06.220 --> 00:53:10.001
Whenever anyone says, "Life is too short,"
I'm like, "Well, life is the longest thing

00:53:10.001 --> 00:53:11.001
you know.

00:53:11.001 --> 00:53:13.970
You don't know anything longer than life."

00:53:13.970 --> 00:53:18.440
So I'm like, "Let's just live it to the fullest
as much as you can."

00:53:18.440 --> 00:53:19.440
You know?

00:53:19.440 --> 00:53:20.440
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, yeah.

00:53:20.440 --> 00:53:21.440
Aryeh Bourkoff: But then do better than that.

00:53:21.440 --> 00:53:28.950
So when you ask someone to define their edge
or their advantage, they'll learn a lot about

00:53:28.950 --> 00:53:30.040
themselves.

00:53:30.040 --> 00:53:32.560
Tim Ferriss: So was this conversation then...

00:53:32.560 --> 00:53:40.470
It was preceding the official performance
review/decision on the bonus?

00:53:40.470 --> 00:53:49.070
This would be a precursor, sort of setting
the table months or weeks or whatever timeframe

00:53:49.070 --> 00:53:50.930
prior to the actual conversation?

00:53:50.930 --> 00:53:51.930
Aryeh Bourkoff: Yes.

00:53:51.930 --> 00:53:55.730
You would see people, and I would rarely even
initiate it, you would see people...

00:53:55.730 --> 00:54:01.770
Oh, I had one this morning, today, actually,
and people wanted to get a sense of where

00:54:01.770 --> 00:54:09.089
they stand or how they're valued, or what
their opportunity is in the future.

00:54:09.089 --> 00:54:13.530
All those are codewords for, "How am I going
to get paid?"

00:54:13.530 --> 00:54:14.530
It's fine.

00:54:14.530 --> 00:54:16.500
It is what it is.

00:54:16.500 --> 00:54:20.790
Again, it would be better for those things
to happen on a day-to-day basis that define

00:54:20.790 --> 00:54:21.790
that instead of my...

00:54:21.790 --> 00:54:22.790
I mean, well, who am I?

00:54:22.790 --> 00:54:24.589
Why am I telling you how you're going to get
paid?

00:54:24.589 --> 00:54:29.300
You tell me how you're going to get paid based
on what your edge and what your opportunity

00:54:29.300 --> 00:54:34.830
is, and my job is to provide the platform
for you to optimize that better than anywhere

00:54:34.830 --> 00:54:40.950
else, and your job is to optimize it here
and have the free market to choose somewhere

00:54:40.950 --> 00:54:47.240
else if you can't do that or I can't do that,
and the bet with all of those solutions or

00:54:47.240 --> 00:54:53.910
scenarios is maybe some biases or some lack
of self-awareness, or my mistakes of not building

00:54:53.910 --> 00:54:55.220
the right platform or not managing correctly.

00:54:55.220 --> 00:54:56.220
All right?

00:54:56.220 --> 00:55:00.470
So, maybe as a manager my job is really to
throw the penalty flags around versus manage

00:55:00.470 --> 00:55:03.579
and tell you exactly what you're worth before
I have to.

00:55:03.579 --> 00:55:09.359
One's entrepreneurial and one's needing to
be secure, but where does that security come

00:55:09.359 --> 00:55:12.079
from, self, inside, or outside?

00:55:12.079 --> 00:55:18.770
When you're a parent to a child, you have
to provide that security, but at some point

00:55:18.770 --> 00:55:20.470
they have to then get unlocked.

00:55:20.470 --> 00:55:26.880
So I just think it's about not everyone has
to be entrepreneurial all the way, but there

00:55:26.880 --> 00:55:31.450
are certain responsibilities that I have to
build the business for people to thrive and

00:55:31.450 --> 00:55:36.200
make sure it's that kind of environment, mission-driven
and opportunistic and a meritocracy around

00:55:36.200 --> 00:55:42.200
that, and certain responsibilities that people
have within it to then take it competitively

00:55:42.200 --> 00:55:50.200
better than the best they can, and the risk
I take is that they could do it somewhere

00:55:50.200 --> 00:55:52.250
else better than here.

00:55:52.250 --> 00:55:54.630
But I own that risk every single day.

00:55:54.630 --> 00:55:57.369
What risk do they own?

00:55:57.369 --> 00:55:59.020
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:55:59.020 --> 00:56:00.020
Yeah.

00:56:00.020 --> 00:56:01.020
Well said.

00:56:01.020 --> 00:56:08.339
So, for people listening, I want to add a
bit of color and then ask about a specific

00:56:08.339 --> 00:56:09.339
instance.

00:56:09.339 --> 00:56:18.950
So, you have been part of and a driving force
behind some of the largest deals the world

00:56:18.950 --> 00:56:27.440
has ever seen, certainly in media, and people
might be listening and thinking, "My god.

00:56:27.440 --> 00:56:28.780
This guy doesn't sleep.

00:56:28.780 --> 00:56:30.280
He has endless energy.

00:56:30.280 --> 00:56:35.410
It's just a home run every time he steps up
to the plate," which may in part be true,

00:56:35.410 --> 00:56:37.070
but could you please...

00:56:37.070 --> 00:56:38.670
I'm going to get the name wrong.

00:56:38.670 --> 00:56:43.190
I don't know how to pronounce this properly,
but can you please tell the deck of cards

00:56:43.190 --> 00:56:45.150
story with Joe Ianniello?

00:56:45.150 --> 00:56:48.420
Is that how you say that name correctly?

00:56:48.420 --> 00:56:49.420
Aryeh Bourkoff: Ianniello.

00:56:49.420 --> 00:56:51.130
Tim Ferriss: I knew I wasn't going to get
that right.

00:56:51.130 --> 00:56:52.130
All right.

00:56:52.130 --> 00:56:55.870
Aryeh Bourkoff: The fact that you got my name
right and not Joe's is a real sign of growth

00:56:55.870 --> 00:56:57.290
in the industry.

00:56:57.290 --> 00:56:59.530
I appreciate it.

00:56:59.530 --> 00:57:04.079
Well, obviously, what appears to be a home
run every time is not.

00:57:04.079 --> 00:57:13.720
I mean, first of all, every day is full of
happiness and successes and frustrations and

00:57:13.720 --> 00:57:17.039
consternations and irritations about what
we could be doing better, what I could be

00:57:17.039 --> 00:57:18.039
doing better, frankly, all the time.

00:57:18.039 --> 00:57:19.039
I do try to...

00:57:19.039 --> 00:57:20.690
I'm as much an introvert as I am an extrovert,
by the way.

00:57:20.690 --> 00:57:24.329
I think the biggest misnomer is I'm always
out and about.

00:57:24.329 --> 00:57:27.910
I get a lot of energy from being out and about
partially because of other people's energy,

00:57:27.910 --> 00:57:34.670
not just my own, but I also really enjoy some
thinking time with music and some reading

00:57:34.670 --> 00:57:42.900
time, and kind of a chance to reenergize and
go deeper than just bouncing around, and that

00:57:42.900 --> 00:57:47.109
you just don't Instagram out the periods we're
you're sitting alone.

00:57:47.109 --> 00:57:48.280
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:57:48.280 --> 00:57:52.089
Aryeh Bourkoff: So people only see you do
the things that you're like where you're bouncing

00:57:52.089 --> 00:57:53.089
around.

00:57:53.089 --> 00:57:56.319
They don't see the moments where you're just
happily sitting alone or reading a book or

00:57:56.319 --> 00:57:58.390
listening, unless you're really not focusing
on it.

00:57:58.390 --> 00:57:59.390
You know?

00:57:59.390 --> 00:58:00.390
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:58:00.390 --> 00:58:03.560
Aryeh Bourkoff: So I think the appearances
are a bit misleading, but it's the texture

00:58:03.560 --> 00:58:09.170
of all of the above, and some of that thoughtfulness
is where you really come into strategizing

00:58:09.170 --> 00:58:15.390
around what the future could be for you, for
your loved ones, and also for the deal making

00:58:15.390 --> 00:58:16.691
in the future.

00:58:16.691 --> 00:58:20.809
But in that particular story, it all goes
back to this element of what you know, what

00:58:20.809 --> 00:58:26.290
you don't know, and we were doing a deal,
I think in 2007, for a company called Sundance

00:58:26.290 --> 00:58:32.589
Channel, which was owned by NBC, CBS, and
Robert Redford, and we were selling the company

00:58:32.589 --> 00:58:33.680
on behalf of the owners.

00:58:33.680 --> 00:58:40.150
I was at UBS, and we really did a good job,
I have to say.

00:58:40.150 --> 00:58:43.240
We did a good job selling the company.

00:58:43.240 --> 00:58:45.970
It was not an easy sale.

00:58:45.970 --> 00:58:48.550
Eventually, the Dolans bought it, actually.

00:58:48.550 --> 00:58:52.880
The company that owned IFC, Cablevision, and
the Knicks bought the Sundance Channel for

00:58:52.880 --> 00:58:56.030
$500 million.

00:58:56.030 --> 00:59:03.690
I thought we did a good job, and I remember
in those days, and in some cases also today,

00:59:03.690 --> 00:59:07.650
when the job is done, then you start to think
about, "Well, how much am I going to be paid

00:59:07.650 --> 00:59:08.650
for the services?"

00:59:08.650 --> 00:59:11.680
Now, sometimes you can get that figured out
at the beginning.

00:59:11.680 --> 00:59:14.859
In this case, you figure it out at the end.

00:59:14.859 --> 00:59:20.299
We were thinking that would work to our benefit
because it was such a home run deal, and I

00:59:20.299 --> 00:59:26.160
thought we did such a good job that they would
want to pay us more, whereas if we agreed

00:59:26.160 --> 00:59:29.460
at the beginning, we probably would've undersold
ourselves.

00:59:29.460 --> 00:59:31.119
Robert Redford did really well in that deal,
I remember.

00:59:31.119 --> 00:59:34.180
I think he probably made more money in that
deal than all of his acting years combined,

00:59:34.180 --> 00:59:37.070
but he was a pleasure to work with.

00:59:37.070 --> 00:59:42.680
But CBS's CFO, or I think it was the treasurer
at the time, was Joe Ianniello.

00:59:42.680 --> 00:59:50.030
He rose through the rank of even being the
CEO for a while after that, and Joe was a

00:59:50.030 --> 00:59:54.349
tough negotiator, but we were on the same
side of the table.

00:59:54.349 --> 00:59:59.960
So, afterwards I was told by my bosses at
UBS, "Okay, Aryeh, great job on the deal.

00:59:59.960 --> 01:00:03.049
Now go get us a good fee," and I said, "Okay.

01:00:03.049 --> 01:00:04.410
I have to do that too?

01:00:04.410 --> 01:00:05.410
All right."

01:00:05.410 --> 01:00:06.410
He said, "Yeah, yeah.

01:00:06.410 --> 01:00:07.930
That's what it takes," and I said, "Okay."

01:00:07.930 --> 01:00:09.859
So, I went over to...

01:00:09.859 --> 01:00:16.240
Joe was appointed as a person that had to
negotiate our fee, and I thought it would

01:00:16.240 --> 01:00:19.680
be like one of these great kumbaya conversations,
we all did a great job together, and let's

01:00:19.680 --> 01:00:23.829
have drinks and get this settled quickly,
but no.

01:00:23.829 --> 01:00:29.700
So I went over to what they call Black Rock,
the CBS building, and it was this long conference

01:00:29.700 --> 01:00:36.400
room, and it was just Joe and myself sitting
in the middle, and Joe said, "Hey, you're

01:00:36.400 --> 01:00:37.722
here to negotiate your fee, right?"

01:00:37.722 --> 01:00:40.520
and I said, "Yeah."

01:00:40.520 --> 01:00:45.900
You project some confidence, but you're really
nervous inside, and he said, "Just to break

01:00:45.900 --> 01:00:49.650
the ice a little bit before we get into that
negotiation, do you want to play some cards?

01:00:49.650 --> 01:00:51.319
Do you want to do a card trick?

01:00:51.319 --> 01:00:53.309
I'm getting into cards recently."

01:00:53.309 --> 01:00:55.579
That sounds great.

01:00:55.579 --> 01:00:57.070
We're friends now too?

01:00:57.070 --> 01:00:58.070
This is great.

01:00:58.070 --> 01:00:59.070
Amazing.

01:00:59.070 --> 01:01:00.730
So, he said, "In fact, you know what?

01:01:00.730 --> 01:01:04.359
More than just a card trick, why don't we
just shortcut the whole thing since you're

01:01:04.359 --> 01:01:11.599
busy, I'm busy, and why don't you just pick
out of the deck any card, and whatever card

01:01:11.599 --> 01:01:12.960
you pick will be your fee, and we'll just
do it by chance."

01:01:12.960 --> 01:01:14.119
I said, "Really?

01:01:14.119 --> 01:01:15.430
We'll do that?"

01:01:15.430 --> 01:01:22.220
I said, "Just to clarify, a face card with
a jack, queen, and king, that'd be like $10

01:01:22.220 --> 01:01:23.280
million?" and he said, "Yeah, that's right.

01:01:23.280 --> 01:01:24.450
Sold the company for 500 million.

01:01:24.450 --> 01:01:25.819
Whatever you pick, that'll be your fee."

01:01:25.819 --> 01:01:27.540
I said, "Okay."

01:01:27.540 --> 01:01:40.020
So, I reach in, I pull a card out, and it's
a three, and he saw my face, that I was very

01:01:40.020 --> 01:01:41.020
disappointed.

01:01:41.020 --> 01:01:45.660
He said, "You seem dejected.

01:01:45.660 --> 01:01:47.930
Would you like to do it again?"

01:01:47.930 --> 01:01:48.930
I said, "Well, yeah.

01:01:48.930 --> 01:01:49.930
Can I do it again?"

01:01:49.930 --> 01:01:50.930
and he said, "Yeah, let's do best out of three."

01:01:50.930 --> 01:01:52.130
So, I said, "Great."

01:01:52.130 --> 01:01:54.710
I'm like, "So nice, gracious."

01:01:54.710 --> 01:02:01.280
I do it again and I pick out a three again,
and I'm thinking, "No, this is terrible."

01:02:01.280 --> 01:02:05.591
Now, I don't remember exactly what I was expecting
to get, but definitely more than a three,

01:02:05.591 --> 01:02:10.390
and definitely relative to the face cards
or anything else I was thinking, this was

01:02:10.390 --> 01:02:12.020
towards the bottom of the deck.

01:02:12.020 --> 01:02:14.490
So, he goes, "You seem like you're in a bit
of a stuck place.

01:02:14.490 --> 01:02:19.820
Maybe you want to call the home office back
at UBS and find out what they have to say."

01:02:19.820 --> 01:02:20.820
I go, "Yeah.

01:02:20.820 --> 01:02:21.820
I have to do that.

01:02:21.820 --> 01:02:22.820
I can't just walk back with that."

01:02:22.820 --> 01:02:24.299
He goes, "You can use the phone on the couch
over here.

01:02:24.299 --> 01:02:25.599
Just sit in the corner."

01:02:25.599 --> 01:02:30.480
So I went to the phone, picked up the handset,
and there was a card on the handset, and it

01:02:30.480 --> 01:02:31.480
was face down.

01:02:31.480 --> 01:02:34.520
I picked it up and I turned it over, and it
was a three.

01:02:34.520 --> 01:02:37.859
By that point, I had realized what was happening.

01:02:37.859 --> 01:02:40.510
I put the phone down —
Tim Ferriss: You were negotiating with David

01:02:40.510 --> 01:02:41.510
Blaine.

01:02:41.510 --> 01:02:44.109
Aryeh Bourkoff: I said, "Are you telling me,
Joe, that our fee is $3 million?"

01:02:44.109 --> 01:02:47.039
He goes, "That's what I'm telling you."

01:02:47.039 --> 01:02:48.112
He had the whole thing wired.

01:02:48.112 --> 01:02:49.380
So I respected it.

01:02:49.380 --> 01:02:51.299
That's what we got.

01:02:51.299 --> 01:02:53.530
That's what we took.

01:02:53.530 --> 01:03:04.789
Tim Ferriss: So negotiating is one of your
sports in the sort of decathlon of dealmaking

01:03:04.789 --> 01:03:09.270
that is the craft, one of the crafts you've
mastered.

01:03:09.270 --> 01:03:12.950
This is going to seem like a lazy question,
but I'm going to ask because it'll be on the

01:03:12.950 --> 01:03:15.520
mind of a lot of people listening.

01:03:15.520 --> 01:03:22.690
For someone who wanted to become a skilled
negotiator, would you have any recommendations

01:03:22.690 --> 01:03:29.900
in terms of resources, approaches, common
beliefs that are actually very unhelpful,

01:03:29.900 --> 01:03:34.279
anything at all for somebody who wants to
develop their ability to negotiate well?

01:03:34.279 --> 01:03:36.270
Aryeh Bourkoff: Yeah, of course.

01:03:36.270 --> 01:03:44.020
As probably the opposite of what people will
believe or instinctively would do, which is

01:03:44.020 --> 01:03:50.650
usually the answer, by the way, I would say
90 percent of the time everyone focused on

01:03:50.650 --> 01:03:56.250
what is important to them and what we or what
you say.

01:03:56.250 --> 01:03:58.980
It's irrelevant.

01:03:58.980 --> 01:04:01.730
All that matters is how it lands to the other
person.

01:04:01.730 --> 01:04:02.730
It's life.

01:04:02.730 --> 01:04:07.069
In order to understand how something will
land to the other person, you have to meet

01:04:07.069 --> 01:04:08.369
them where they are.

01:04:08.369 --> 01:04:11.890
You have to put yourself in their shoes.

01:04:11.890 --> 01:04:16.270
Once you put yourself in someone else's shoes,
then you don't have to do what they say in

01:04:16.270 --> 01:04:22.220
a negotiation, but you have to at least start
with that understanding, and then go back

01:04:22.220 --> 01:04:27.970
to what's most important to you from there,
and then you have a fair understanding of

01:04:27.970 --> 01:04:30.320
what that negotiation is going to look like.

01:04:30.320 --> 01:04:35.160
But if you just start throwing your words
against the wall, it's never going to end

01:04:35.160 --> 01:04:40.260
well, and you're going to be very frustrated,
and there'll be temper tantrums, and so on.

01:04:40.260 --> 01:04:41.349
I kind of make it...

01:04:41.349 --> 01:04:45.990
The best analogy I can think of is when you
are in huddle, about to throw at pass at a

01:04:45.990 --> 01:04:50.730
football game, and you have figured out you're
going to throw a post pattern in the huddle,

01:04:50.730 --> 01:04:53.630
and you thought about in that huddle.

01:04:53.630 --> 01:05:00.680
If you say "Hike," play goes, and you throw
the post pattern on play, but you haven't

01:05:00.680 --> 01:05:05.750
looked at actually what happened in the field,
and the receiver had to go in a different

01:05:05.750 --> 01:05:11.000
direction, you need to meet that receiver
where they are.

01:05:11.000 --> 01:05:12.779
Your post pattern play is irrelevant.

01:05:12.779 --> 01:05:17.390
It's only relevant to your formation at the
beginning, but it's not relevant to actually

01:05:17.390 --> 01:05:21.089
where the ball is going to go in the end,
because what happened on the field is much

01:05:21.089 --> 01:05:22.830
more important.

01:05:22.830 --> 01:05:24.839
So, you have to do the preparation work.

01:05:24.839 --> 01:05:29.410
You have to have a sense of what you think
is proper and right from your perspective,

01:05:29.410 --> 01:05:34.619
but you also have to understand what will
be the scenarios that will be important to

01:05:34.619 --> 01:05:39.700
them, and then the middle ground approach
is too often used.

01:05:39.700 --> 01:05:40.700
It's not that.

01:05:40.700 --> 01:05:43.210
It's just starting from understanding both
vantage points.

01:05:43.210 --> 01:05:46.369
It's the same kind of skill set as saying
before I put a buy on something, I have to

01:05:46.369 --> 01:05:49.099
also reject the sell.

01:05:49.099 --> 01:05:55.250
You have to be comfortable in an unknown position
before you can adequately argue for what's

01:05:55.250 --> 01:05:56.250
important to you.

01:05:56.250 --> 01:06:01.789
Then in a trusted relationship, which is the
only way to negotiate, in a trusted relationship,

01:06:01.789 --> 01:06:04.770
you're going to get down to things that are
really important and things that are not really

01:06:04.770 --> 01:06:09.500
that important, and to be able to be comfortable
saying that...

01:06:09.500 --> 01:06:10.500
Okay.

01:06:10.500 --> 01:06:16.160
If I listed these three things, please rank
in order for me what's most important to you

01:06:16.160 --> 01:06:22.150
and what you really don't need, that would
give me a little bit more room to come back

01:06:22.150 --> 01:06:25.829
to you with something that I think we can
work with, because that shows that you'll

01:06:25.829 --> 01:06:31.180
be willing to give up something to get something,
which is obviously a hallmark in any transaction.

01:06:31.180 --> 01:06:32.980
Now that's a good negotiation.

01:06:32.980 --> 01:06:35.609
There are tactics above that.

01:06:35.609 --> 01:06:40.079
So, sometimes in order to get to somewhere
that you need, you may ask for things that

01:06:40.079 --> 01:06:48.119
you don't really want just to settle back
into something that is a discount to that,

01:06:48.119 --> 01:06:52.910
that you only would do that if you feel like
the other person's doing the same thing.

01:06:52.910 --> 01:06:58.520
So it all goes back to trust, and that trust
is not about one person trusting another.

01:06:58.520 --> 01:07:02.640
It's the different phases of the negotiation
that trust picks up.

01:07:02.640 --> 01:07:06.970
So, at the beginning of a negotiation, it
may be less trustworthy than at the end when

01:07:06.970 --> 01:07:10.840
things are really coming to the finish line,
when you can see the light of day.

01:07:10.840 --> 01:07:13.230
Then things start to pick up towards the trust.

01:07:13.230 --> 01:07:18.480
So, you have to see when you can play with
different tactics, but at the end of the day,

01:07:18.480 --> 01:07:21.450
it's seeing people where they are, I think
is really, really important, without giving

01:07:21.450 --> 01:07:22.470
up what you really need.

01:07:22.470 --> 01:07:24.369
Tim Ferriss: So let's explore that a bit.

01:07:24.369 --> 01:07:28.980
So, seeing people where they are, you gave
the football metaphor.

01:07:28.980 --> 01:07:39.270
Could you describe an actual deal situation
or process?

01:07:39.270 --> 01:07:40.270
It could be real.

01:07:40.270 --> 01:07:41.609
It could be anonymized.

01:07:41.609 --> 01:07:49.869
It could be hypothetical, but just to walk
us through a concrete case study of what that

01:07:49.869 --> 01:07:55.200
looks like, knowing the other side, since
you led with that in your answer.

01:07:55.200 --> 01:07:57.280
Could you perhaps just walk us through an
example?

01:07:57.280 --> 01:07:58.280
Aryeh Bourkoff: Yeah.

01:07:58.280 --> 01:07:59.280
I will, actually.

01:07:59.280 --> 01:08:06.260
I think all the deals that we work on that
are public, company deals, end up being filed

01:08:06.260 --> 01:08:09.900
publicly with the play-by-play of the deal.

01:08:09.900 --> 01:08:16.890
No one likes to read legal documents, but
the fun part of the legal document is the

01:08:16.890 --> 01:08:22.730
play-by-play of the deal if people are interested
in the step-by-step of how a deal works, because

01:08:22.730 --> 01:08:27.799
while we're doing the deal, we're very aware
of the fact that it will be filed publicly.

01:08:27.799 --> 01:08:35.720
So, it's not like you're pulling fast ones
because it's going to be disclosed, how you're

01:08:35.720 --> 01:08:37.040
doing what you're doing.

01:08:37.040 --> 01:08:38.700
So, you have to be truthful.

01:08:38.700 --> 01:08:43.740
There's no concept of lying in a deal because
it will all be uncovered later, if you want

01:08:43.740 --> 01:08:46.859
to be in the business the next day.

01:08:46.859 --> 01:08:53.150
So that's called a proxy statement, and that
one section is written up by lawyers that

01:08:53.150 --> 01:08:54.759
actually recount the deal.

01:08:54.759 --> 01:08:55.960
Tim Ferriss: Did not know that.

01:08:55.960 --> 01:08:56.960
Aryeh Bourkoff: Yeah.

01:08:56.960 --> 01:08:59.000
So, what I'm going to say is public information.

01:08:59.000 --> 01:09:05.920
When we did the transaction of selling MGM
Studios to Amazon, which is the only example

01:09:05.920 --> 01:09:14.330
of a content company being sold to a technology
company to date, there was an impasse between

01:09:14.330 --> 01:09:19.029
the buyer and the seller that went on for
a while.

01:09:19.029 --> 01:09:27.780
The deal ended up being worth around $8.5
billion, but for months Amazon would not go

01:09:27.780 --> 01:09:35.839
above eight billion, and MGM and the owners
of MGM decided, "We are not selling below

01:09:35.839 --> 01:09:38.040
$9 billion, hell or high water."

01:09:38.040 --> 01:09:44.489
So we were at an eight-to-nine-billion-dollar
bid ask for months, to the point where the

01:09:44.489 --> 01:09:46.069
deal could've gone away.

01:09:46.069 --> 01:09:51.560
On the one hand you're close, on the other
hand, nothing's happening.

01:09:51.560 --> 01:09:59.199
So, finally, the chairman on MGM who was a
really dear friend calls me and says, "I'm

01:09:59.199 --> 01:10:00.199
ready to...

01:10:00.199 --> 01:10:01.199
What do you think?

01:10:01.199 --> 01:10:07.280
I'm ready to unlock this gap," and I knew
Amazon very well, enough to know that they

01:10:07.280 --> 01:10:12.880
were thinking because of this gap was going
on for so long that the owner of MGM and the

01:10:12.880 --> 01:10:16.570
chairman, in their mind, they were thinking
didn't really want to sell.

01:10:16.570 --> 01:10:21.460
So they were thinking that nine billion was
just basically saying, "I don't really want

01:10:21.460 --> 01:10:22.460
to sell."

01:10:22.460 --> 01:10:26.489
Of course, I knew that was not to be the case.

01:10:26.489 --> 01:10:34.989
So the owner and chairman of MGM said to me,
"I give you, my proxy, to go in there and

01:10:34.989 --> 01:10:36.420
break the log jam."

01:10:36.420 --> 01:10:41.880
So I did, and when I went to call the Amazon
executives, I said, "Okay.

01:10:41.880 --> 01:10:45.150
We're ready to negotiate now," and they said,
"Well, there's nothing to negotiate.

01:10:45.150 --> 01:10:49.060
They're at nine billion, and we're at eight
billion, and we're not moving."

01:10:49.060 --> 01:10:50.350
I said, "Okay."

01:10:50.350 --> 01:10:53.699
They said, "They're not even really interested
in selling the company."

01:10:53.699 --> 01:10:57.090
I said, "Well, what if I told you that they
were selling the company?"

01:10:57.090 --> 01:10:58.659
He said, "Well, how would I know?"

01:10:58.659 --> 01:11:05.490
I said, "Well, I'll, in good faith, speak
on behalf of the chairman, and I give you

01:11:05.490 --> 01:11:15.679
a new offer that will yield a bit, under the
condition that you get back to me with a counteroffer

01:11:15.679 --> 01:11:23.940
right away, within 24 to 48 hours, so I get
also the feedback back to the chairman that

01:11:23.940 --> 01:11:29.851
you're serious about dealings, meaning that
if I could speak for a principal, you have

01:11:29.851 --> 01:11:36.401
to speak also that you want to get something
done, but I'm not going to move unless you're

01:11:36.401 --> 01:11:38.654
going to move, and I need know that you're
going to move."

01:11:38.654 --> 01:11:43.060
That was trust because they took a risk that
I could represent the owners of MGM.

01:11:43.060 --> 01:11:44.770
They said, "Okay.

01:11:44.770 --> 01:11:51.230
What do you got?" and I said, "I think we
should split the difference and go right to

01:11:51.230 --> 01:11:57.580
it, but it has to go fast, and you have to
get back to me right away," and they did.

01:11:57.580 --> 01:11:59.929
If they didn't...

01:11:59.929 --> 01:12:06.239
In those 48 hours, there was a lot of emotion,
but they did get back, and it moved very,

01:12:06.239 --> 01:12:07.580
very fast from there.

01:12:07.580 --> 01:12:11.050
So that's exactly what we're talking about.

01:12:11.050 --> 01:12:12.540
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:12:12.540 --> 01:12:17.679
Well, someday I look forward to reading your
compendium of deal stories.

01:12:17.679 --> 01:12:21.600
I would be the first in line to buy a copy
of that.

01:12:21.600 --> 01:12:27.739
But before we get to the compendium of deal
stories, probably a better title to be had,

01:12:27.739 --> 01:12:33.800
you mentioned a while back reading time, and
there are quite a few books recommended in

01:12:33.800 --> 01:12:37.190
your year-end letter.

01:12:37.190 --> 01:12:41.989
Are there any particular books that come to
mind that have impacted your thinking recently,

01:12:41.989 --> 01:12:50.060
in the last few years, or any, let's just
say timeless books, perennial books that have

01:12:50.060 --> 01:12:51.060
impacted your thinking?

01:12:51.060 --> 01:12:52.060
Aryeh Bourkoff: Yeah.

01:12:52.060 --> 01:12:53.060
There are quite a few.

01:12:53.060 --> 01:12:56.469
I mean, I probably buy more books than I read.

01:12:56.469 --> 01:12:59.389
I wonder if that's the case for a lot of people,
actually.

01:12:59.389 --> 01:13:02.239
I listen to more books than I read.

01:13:02.239 --> 01:13:09.139
I don't read complete books, but I do try
to read as much as I can.

01:13:09.139 --> 01:13:13.389
So I would say the timeless books are, one
we'll call Leadership and Self-Deception.

01:13:13.389 --> 01:13:21.449
It's really a book about self-awareness that
we really handed out to the firm, and it's

01:13:21.449 --> 01:13:25.250
a book about really just owning your own decisions.

01:13:25.250 --> 01:13:27.060
That's a timeless book.

01:13:27.060 --> 01:13:29.400
Tim Ferriss: And the title was Leadership
and Self-Deception?

01:13:29.400 --> 01:13:32.270
Aryeh Bourkoff: Yeah, Leadership and Self-Deception.

01:13:32.270 --> 01:13:39.909
The other two timeless books are a book called
Scale by Geoffrey West that is all about why

01:13:39.909 --> 01:13:42.409
some things last minutes.

01:13:42.409 --> 01:13:46.920
Humans could go on for 70, 80 years.

01:13:46.920 --> 01:13:49.350
The Queen lasted 96 years.

01:13:49.350 --> 01:13:54.770
The average public company goes for 10, 10-and-a-half
years, and then dies, but cities seemingly

01:13:54.770 --> 01:13:55.770
last forever.

01:13:55.770 --> 01:13:59.800
You can throw nuclear bombs at cities, unfortunately,
but they still keep moving.

01:13:59.800 --> 01:14:04.989
Why do some things last finite periods of
time, and some things last much longer?

01:14:04.989 --> 01:14:11.560
It's all about what you feed it and the ability
to grow it, but ultimately, it goes back to

01:14:11.560 --> 01:14:12.560
adaptability.

01:14:12.560 --> 01:14:19.020
If something has a rigid purpose and you break
the purpose, it will die.

01:14:19.020 --> 01:14:20.670
Profits for a company will die.

01:14:20.670 --> 01:14:23.700
A city, the people always reinvent itself.

01:14:23.700 --> 01:14:31.530
This summer, I think five governments fell
this summer, the UK, you had Italy, you had

01:14:31.530 --> 01:14:38.390
Israel, you had Sweden recently, and you have
even France became dysfunctional, and I was

01:14:38.390 --> 01:14:39.620
like, "Oh, my goodness.

01:14:39.620 --> 01:14:43.080
All these governments are falling, but that's
okay because if the people are unhappy and

01:14:43.080 --> 01:14:45.900
the governments don't fall, that's rigid.

01:14:45.900 --> 01:14:47.520
That's a problem."

01:14:47.520 --> 01:14:54.130
If governments fall, it's a chance for reinvention,
and just like New York City where I'm sitting

01:14:54.130 --> 01:15:02.219
now had a chance to reinvent itself post-pandemic,
or in Detroit, a company town, when the industry

01:15:02.219 --> 01:15:09.750
falters in the auto industry, it had a chance
to reinvent itself with creativity, or Medellín

01:15:09.750 --> 01:15:15.640
in Colombia, it used to be known as a rough
drug trade town.

01:15:15.640 --> 01:15:19.520
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, murder capital of the world
during Escobar's rule.

01:15:19.520 --> 01:15:20.520
Yeah.

01:15:20.520 --> 01:15:21.520
Aryeh Bourkoff: Yeah.

01:15:21.520 --> 01:15:22.520
That was cool.

01:15:22.520 --> 01:15:23.520
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:15:23.520 --> 01:15:24.710
Aryeh Bourkoff: Technology hub, artsy.

01:15:24.710 --> 01:15:28.820
Cities reinvent themselves, basically adaptable,
forcing function of those people.

01:15:28.820 --> 01:15:30.220
I love that.

01:15:30.220 --> 01:15:32.560
It's very helpful for a company build dynamic.

01:15:32.560 --> 01:15:36.889
The third one is a book called Scarcity.

01:15:36.889 --> 01:15:41.090
I think we're moving from a period of abundance
to scarcity.

01:15:41.090 --> 01:15:48.560
So how you are not fearful of scarcity, but
how you could do more with less, and we're

01:15:48.560 --> 01:15:54.310
getting into a period now where unfortunately,
there's scarcity of food and water and energy

01:15:54.310 --> 01:16:02.989
resources, shortages because of macroeconomic
dynamics, warfare in the Ukraine and Russia,

01:16:02.989 --> 01:16:08.630
and I think inequality post-pandemic and how
you deal with scarce resources, people paying

01:16:08.630 --> 01:16:13.600
their bills plus inflation, what you do out
of those dynamics, it creates a lot of tension

01:16:13.600 --> 01:16:18.370
and fear and conflict, even military conflict.

01:16:18.370 --> 01:16:24.180
It's a different kind of read than Abundance,
and we have everything we want.

01:16:24.180 --> 01:16:28.230
But then the books I read now more recently
are about people.

01:16:28.230 --> 01:16:35.300
I'm reading a book now called A Banker's Journey
about Edmond Safra's journey, a banker from

01:16:35.300 --> 01:16:36.300
Brazil.

01:16:36.300 --> 01:16:37.300
It just came out.

01:16:37.300 --> 01:16:43.453
I'm reading a book now by Chris Blackwell
about the music industry and his forming in

01:16:43.453 --> 01:16:48.540
the music industry and founding Bob Marley
and U2, and his journey, things that are interesting,

01:16:48.540 --> 01:16:49.560
personal stories.

01:16:49.560 --> 01:16:51.270
I read books all the time.

01:16:51.270 --> 01:16:55.480
Jann Wenner just came out with a book today
I'm looking forward to buying, the Rolling

01:16:55.480 --> 01:16:57.360
Stone editor and founder.

01:16:57.360 --> 01:17:01.870
He and his son Gus are friends, his son more
than Jann.

01:17:01.870 --> 01:17:06.270
But interesting life stories, texture to read.

01:17:06.270 --> 01:17:10.929
I like to read a lot and I probably read more
than I consume other forms of media.

01:17:10.929 --> 01:17:18.580
Tim Ferriss: So related to the books, any
biographies that really stick out for you?

01:17:18.580 --> 01:17:23.989
And then second, if you read any fiction,
which is not a judgment question, but if you

01:17:23.989 --> 01:17:28.190
read any fiction, if there are any fiction
books that have had an outsized impact on

01:17:28.190 --> 01:17:29.440
you in any way?

01:17:29.440 --> 01:17:30.530
Aryeh Bourkoff: Biographies?

01:17:30.530 --> 01:17:32.830
I like all the Niall Ferguson books.

01:17:32.830 --> 01:17:37.929
I read The House of Rothschild, the J.P. Morgan
books.

01:17:37.929 --> 01:17:44.340
I actually read Bill Cohan's book on the Lazard,
Last Tycoons.

01:17:44.340 --> 01:17:45.390
Really good book.

01:17:45.390 --> 01:17:50.390
That's not only because of being in this business,
but you do want to go back to the underpinnings

01:17:50.390 --> 01:17:53.290
of what is relationship banking.

01:17:53.290 --> 01:17:59.360
I really think that's a really interesting
profession beyond just finance.

01:17:59.360 --> 01:18:06.010
It's how to build with people over a long
period of time, I think, with outsized impact.

01:18:06.010 --> 01:18:11.100
I think that's really, really interesting
to me and I hope other people in the firm

01:18:11.100 --> 01:18:12.280
and even your listenership.

01:18:12.280 --> 01:18:13.280
And fiction?

01:18:13.280 --> 01:18:17.000
I've always loved some of the mystery novels,
the legal thrillers.

01:18:17.000 --> 01:18:19.780
I've always liked the John Grisham books.

01:18:19.780 --> 01:18:23.570
But I would say meaningful to me?

01:18:23.570 --> 01:18:28.239
Ayn Rand, obviously it's not fiction per se.

01:18:28.239 --> 01:18:32.840
John Steinbeck, The Pearl, a recommendation
of a friend of mine who's in the business.

01:18:32.840 --> 01:18:37.490
I reread the other day, great book.

01:18:37.490 --> 01:18:39.679
And I love The Sun Also Rises, Hemingway.

01:18:39.679 --> 01:18:41.160
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, great book.

01:18:41.160 --> 01:18:46.130
Aryeh Bourkoff: There's a particular line
in there that I love, at least if we're talking

01:18:46.130 --> 01:18:52.110
about bankruptcy, saying that it happens gradually
and then suddenly.

01:18:52.110 --> 01:19:00.160
Sometimes things on the way up happen to feel
very slow and methodical, but on the way down,

01:19:00.160 --> 01:19:02.030
things happen gradually and then suddenly.

01:19:02.030 --> 01:19:05.300
There's not a symmetric relationship between
things that happen on the way up and things

01:19:05.300 --> 01:19:06.690
that happen on the way down.

01:19:06.690 --> 01:19:10.730
And then another book I would say is David
Brooks' The Second Mountain.

01:19:10.730 --> 01:19:12.980
It's a really interesting book.

01:19:12.980 --> 01:19:22.980
I view it as a book about personal development,
careers, people in our age of life.

01:19:22.980 --> 01:19:28.270
You kind of start your career coming out of
school and thinking about what you want to

01:19:28.270 --> 01:19:29.460
do, one step in front of the other.

01:19:29.460 --> 01:19:34.280
And then you get to a certain point at our
stage of life and then you say, "Well that's

01:19:34.280 --> 01:19:36.100
where I started from to where I am.

01:19:36.100 --> 01:19:40.920
It has nothing really to do with where I want
to end up, top of the second mountain."

01:19:40.920 --> 01:19:45.250
And once you start thinking about that, you
end up in the valley of thought until you

01:19:45.250 --> 01:19:47.150
figure out where that second mountain is.

01:19:47.150 --> 01:19:51.980
And when you start climbing the second mountain,
you don't even have to get to the destination.

01:19:51.980 --> 01:19:53.790
It's just a joyful climb.

01:19:53.790 --> 01:19:55.440
It's a happy climb.

01:19:55.440 --> 01:19:58.760
You're doing every single thing you want to
do with every breath you have.

01:19:58.760 --> 01:20:01.450
I love that whole concept, finding that second
mountain.

01:20:01.450 --> 01:20:06.620
There's no such thing as a linear move from
the day you come out of school all the way

01:20:06.620 --> 01:20:07.620
until you die.

01:20:07.620 --> 01:20:08.620
It's not linear.

01:20:08.620 --> 01:20:13.770
You're going to hit a chasm and it's cool
until you find that second mountain to climb

01:20:13.770 --> 01:20:15.380
personally, professionally, et cetera.

01:20:15.380 --> 01:20:16.600
It's all good.

01:20:16.600 --> 01:20:19.680
Tim Ferriss: I'm going to buy that today.

01:20:19.680 --> 01:20:27.510
I also love John Steinbeck and I've read pitifully
little of his work, but Travels with Charley,

01:20:27.510 --> 01:20:34.390
if anyone has not read that nonfiction account
of him traveling across the United States

01:20:34.390 --> 01:20:41.660
with his dog, it is absolutely laugh-out-loud
hilarious, and also very profound in its hilarious

01:20:41.660 --> 01:20:42.660
observations.

01:20:42.660 --> 01:20:44.990
But David Brooks, I'm going to get that book
today.

01:20:44.990 --> 01:20:46.969
Thank you for the recommendation.

01:20:46.969 --> 01:20:53.409
I must ask a question to scratch my own itch
in terms of curiosity, and this is related

01:20:53.409 --> 01:20:57.810
to one of your more recent investments.

01:20:57.810 --> 01:21:01.830
You've invested in Malcolm Gladwell's audio
production company, Pushkin Industries, per

01:21:01.830 --> 01:21:03.850
your 2021 year end letter.

01:21:03.850 --> 01:21:06.690
I'm a huge fan of Malcolm.

01:21:06.690 --> 01:21:12.060
He's been on the podcast, huge fan of Michael
Lewis also who's been involved with Pushkin

01:21:12.060 --> 01:21:15.290
and who's also been on the podcast.

01:21:15.290 --> 01:21:23.310
The caliber and quality of what they produce
is I think absolutely astonishing.

01:21:23.310 --> 01:21:26.050
What do you think is next for the podcast
market?

01:21:26.050 --> 01:21:31.370
Or if you have any thoughts on where you think
things are going, what trends are emerging

01:21:31.370 --> 01:21:33.120
or converging?

01:21:33.120 --> 01:21:39.280
What do you see coming over the next handful
of years for the podcast market or industry?

01:21:39.280 --> 01:21:45.380
Aryeh Bourkoff: The audio market and the podcast
market is strong and growing.

01:21:45.380 --> 01:21:51.639
Clearly, like everything in content, the quality
has to rise to the top because a lot of people

01:21:51.639 --> 01:21:58.850
have jumped into it and have to also get curated
in terms of guides.

01:21:58.850 --> 01:22:03.210
Remember at the beginning of YouTube you were
in the jungle, you had no idea how to navigate

01:22:03.210 --> 01:22:04.210
YouTube.

01:22:04.210 --> 01:22:08.110
Now finally you could, if you want to go to
sports you know where to go.

01:22:08.110 --> 01:22:12.390
And if you want to go to history, if you want
to go to brands, commerce, you know where

01:22:12.390 --> 01:22:13.390
to go.

01:22:13.390 --> 01:22:18.870
So I think there needs to be more curation
around audio and podcasting in general, where

01:22:18.870 --> 01:22:26.520
you find different content and when, how you
digest highlights versus the whole episodes,

01:22:26.520 --> 01:22:28.350
et cetera.

01:22:28.350 --> 01:22:32.520
But if it's going to be more branded and more
curated I think from here.

01:22:32.520 --> 01:22:37.210
And heavily monetized, but hopefully not over-monetized
in ways that are uncomfortable.

01:22:37.210 --> 01:22:42.220
I'm not anticipating things that are full
advertisements, but sponsorship is going to

01:22:42.220 --> 01:22:44.570
be more interesting.

01:22:44.570 --> 01:22:49.600
Platforms that will really get behind it.

01:22:49.600 --> 01:22:57.280
Obviously iHeart, Spotify, Amazon, but every
platform that reaches a global audience needs

01:22:57.280 --> 01:22:59.530
differentiated content today.

01:22:59.530 --> 01:23:04.929
Video content alone has hit diminishing returns.

01:23:04.929 --> 01:23:11.070
Audio content and podcasting content, I think,
that's really well-branded and curated and

01:23:11.070 --> 01:23:14.400
quality like this one, I think the best is
yet to come still.

01:23:14.400 --> 01:23:17.300
And it hasn't even stretched service globally.

01:23:17.300 --> 01:23:20.900
So I think those are going to be really interesting.

01:23:20.900 --> 01:23:26.920
But I think more curated, more guides, more
branded content and monetized in different

01:23:26.920 --> 01:23:29.420
ways I think will be pretty important.

01:23:29.420 --> 01:23:35.930
And then there's social features as well,
some more interactivity of being able to do

01:23:35.930 --> 01:23:42.090
events and tours and going around to talk
to your audiences and having more of an interaction

01:23:42.090 --> 01:23:44.990
between the fans and the creators I think
is a big wide space.

01:23:44.990 --> 01:23:49.300
Tim Ferriss: And do you have any thoughts
on how the market will move or consolidate?

01:23:49.300 --> 01:23:57.940
Because I've watched all this with great interest
of course, and I've looked at certain transactions,

01:23:57.940 --> 01:24:01.120
say relatively speaking, heavy spending.

01:24:01.120 --> 01:24:06.340
And I will just underscore something you said,
which is, I think it's still very early.

01:24:06.340 --> 01:24:14.100
If you look at the terrestrial radio, total
ad dollars compared to what has currently

01:24:14.100 --> 01:24:18.389
migrated to say podcast, it is still just
the tip of the iceberg.

01:24:18.389 --> 01:24:23.230
And even when I launched this podcast in,
I can't even recall what it was, 2014, 2016,

01:24:23.230 --> 01:24:26.950
I was told by folks the ship has sailed, it's
too late, there are too many podcasts.

01:24:26.950 --> 01:24:28.810
I just don't think that's the case at all.

01:24:28.810 --> 01:24:33.210
Aryeh Bourkoff: I was told the same thing
when we launched the bank in 2012, that it

01:24:33.210 --> 01:24:37.219
was late to start a new independent bank,
that ship has sailed.

01:24:37.219 --> 01:24:41.369
And I was like, "Well, maybe the door will
shut behind us, we'll be the last one."

01:24:41.369 --> 01:24:45.840
Tim Ferriss: Seems it seems to be working
out in both cases.

01:24:45.840 --> 01:24:52.770
And if I pick one player, for instance, let's
just say Spotify spending very heavily with

01:24:52.770 --> 01:24:59.430
respect to just their market cap and moving
into podcasts, the Joe Rogan deal valued at

01:24:59.430 --> 01:25:05.190
least a 100 million, probably quite a bit
more with earnouts and everything else, my

01:25:05.190 --> 01:25:12.920
assumption has always been that once Spotify
accounts for more than single-digit listenership

01:25:12.920 --> 01:25:20.570
or downloads or streams for the top, say 100
podcasts which will be reflective of broader

01:25:20.570 --> 01:25:28.460
trends, that that type of talent acquisition
would drop quite significantly.

01:25:28.460 --> 01:25:31.489
Just as one sort of hypothesis.

01:25:31.489 --> 01:25:36.119
But what do you think the movements will be
amongst some of the larger players?

01:25:36.119 --> 01:25:44.469
Because you have Amazon, you have Spotify,
you have iHeart and podcasts are an existential

01:25:44.469 --> 01:25:49.940
imperative perhaps or viewed that way for
some and not others.

01:25:49.940 --> 01:25:57.929
What do you think the landscape of the larger
players will look like a few years from now?

01:25:57.929 --> 01:26:02.870
Aryeh Bourkoff: Look, I think it took some
of the streaming platforms like Netflix and

01:26:02.870 --> 01:26:09.510
Amazon and Apple a long time to get into sports,
for example.

01:26:09.510 --> 01:26:15.850
And now they're all bidding for sports rights
because they have to have sports as a differentiator.

01:26:15.850 --> 01:26:17.639
And once one gets in, they're all in.

01:26:17.639 --> 01:26:23.060
So Amazon is bidding for sports rights, Apple
just did the MLS deal, and they're all looking

01:26:23.060 --> 01:26:24.060
at sports.

01:26:24.060 --> 01:26:27.040
Netflix has Formula 1: Drive to Survive, right?

01:26:27.040 --> 01:26:30.139
In some ways, I think podcasting is the same
thing.

01:26:30.139 --> 01:26:37.340
Now that it's hitting the sweet spot of the
curve from a revenue and profitability contribution

01:26:37.340 --> 01:26:44.150
for iHeart, that was all over Spotify's investor
day of the podcasting growth driver.

01:26:44.150 --> 01:26:50.050
Then you're going to see Amazon and all of
them jump into it and then you may see competition

01:26:50.050 --> 01:26:58.179
for talent between platforms and then you
may start to see some of the podcasting personalities

01:26:58.179 --> 01:27:00.790
and brands intersect with other genres.

01:27:00.790 --> 01:27:08.659
So imagine you're with Amazon and Amazon's
doing the NFL and they want your post-game

01:27:08.659 --> 01:27:13.830
show intersected with your podcast of the
NFL.

01:27:13.830 --> 01:27:19.280
So they're going to start using you as a key
brand for the platform overall beyond just

01:27:19.280 --> 01:27:20.280
the show.

01:27:20.280 --> 01:27:23.350
So I think that these becomes assets of the
platform that become very interesting and

01:27:23.350 --> 01:27:25.600
different packaging of the deals I think will
be fascinating.

01:27:25.600 --> 01:27:33.000
It all depends on the loyalty and engagement
of your user base and your community and what

01:27:33.000 --> 01:27:37.480
the community is going to be driving that
they want to see and obviously your ability

01:27:37.480 --> 01:27:38.670
to engage with them.

01:27:38.670 --> 01:27:45.410
Tim Ferriss: I hadn't thought about the cross-asset
utilization of podcast personalities, but

01:27:45.410 --> 01:27:52.320
that makes all the sense in the world and
I have to imagine that's playing into negotiations

01:27:52.320 --> 01:27:56.949
right now for talent deals because they might
say, "Well, what's the size of your advertising

01:27:56.949 --> 01:28:00.540
base?

01:28:00.540 --> 01:28:06.600
Will your sponsors transfer over to us if
we acquire exclusive license or if we acquire

01:28:06.600 --> 01:28:07.600
you outright?"

01:28:07.600 --> 01:28:14.290
Or whatever it might be, but in fact the value
of that property in terms of the personality

01:28:14.290 --> 01:28:21.949
may be much greater when it's viewed as being
possibly applied to all these various things.

01:28:21.949 --> 01:28:29.300
This is pure speculation, not investment advice,
obviously, but I think Twitch is going to

01:28:29.300 --> 01:28:34.619
prove to be increasingly valuable to Amazon.

01:28:34.619 --> 01:28:42.719
And I recall when the pandemic hit, I had
an ongoing bet of sorts with a friend of mine

01:28:42.719 --> 01:28:50.810
that we would see some type of platform utilization
for streaming sports if sports could not be

01:28:50.810 --> 01:28:52.560
attended in person.

01:28:52.560 --> 01:28:58.070
And I just think these off-label uses of these
technology platforms and cross-asset utilization

01:28:58.070 --> 01:29:01.600
of personalities, I hadn't thought about that,
makes a whole lot of sense.

01:29:01.600 --> 01:29:05.780
Aryeh Bourkoff: You and I met at Formula 1,
so imagine maybe that's in your future where

01:29:05.780 --> 01:29:11.679
you're taking on a British accent, doing the
Formula 1 car, I know you're friends with

01:29:11.679 --> 01:29:14.969
Lewis and I think there could be that in your
future.

01:29:14.969 --> 01:29:20.909
But what do you want, if you were going to
migrate and take and evolve your audience

01:29:20.909 --> 01:29:25.600
into a different genre with you, what do you
think that they would be into?

01:29:25.600 --> 01:29:27.170
Tim Ferriss: That's a great question.

01:29:27.170 --> 01:29:32.020
I haven't really thought of it in this way.

01:29:32.020 --> 01:29:39.219
I would imagine, and this will seem very simplistic,
but whatever I am interested in, I think my

01:29:39.219 --> 01:29:45.040
audience will trust me to take a very close
look at, or a meaningful percentage of my

01:29:45.040 --> 01:29:50.870
audience will trust me to look at, which is
by design largely, that's why I did a book

01:29:50.870 --> 01:29:55.560
on physical and sports performance after The
4-Hour Workweek rather than doing The 3-Hour

01:29:55.560 --> 01:30:02.179
Workweek, is I wanted to see early on, since
I had this open option to always extend the

01:30:02.179 --> 01:30:07.170
business book side of things, if my audience,
much like say with a Michael Lewis, even though

01:30:07.170 --> 01:30:14.389
he's a far superior writer, or John McPhee,
or Malcolm Gladwell, would they follow me

01:30:14.389 --> 01:30:17.600
because of my approach and thinking, not because
of the subject matter?

01:30:17.600 --> 01:30:21.380
And I think by and large that has held since
then.

01:30:21.380 --> 01:30:28.520
So it would be a matter of me determining
what I am legitimately interested in because

01:30:28.520 --> 01:30:34.239
without that legitimate interest, I won't
have the endurance, I won't have the necessary

01:30:34.239 --> 01:30:40.340
emotional investment and endurance to make
it count in any meaningful way, if that makes

01:30:40.340 --> 01:30:41.340
sense.

01:30:41.340 --> 01:30:46.679
Aryeh Bourkoff: Well yeah, I think that your
whole community is built on trust.

01:30:46.679 --> 01:30:56.370
You also have a curiosity to you as well,
but based on certain foundations that people

01:30:56.370 --> 01:30:58.340
view you as dependable.

01:30:58.340 --> 01:31:04.449
They don't think you're going to veer them
off to an extreme position, but you're inquisitive

01:31:04.449 --> 01:31:06.159
about where the world's going.

01:31:06.159 --> 01:31:09.699
And my view is that you're ultimately interested
in an impact around that.

01:31:09.699 --> 01:31:10.699
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I am.

01:31:10.699 --> 01:31:12.310
Aryeh Bourkoff: But not in an establishment
sort of way.

01:31:12.310 --> 01:31:14.159
There's this sort of insurgency around it,
right?

01:31:14.159 --> 01:31:15.159
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:31:15.159 --> 01:31:16.860
Aryeh Bourkoff: I think we're very similar.

01:31:16.860 --> 01:31:19.139
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I do too.

01:31:19.139 --> 01:31:23.910
I was just thinking about this trust and how
its asymmetrical rise, decline, the precipitous

01:31:23.910 --> 01:31:33.639
drop, how it takes 20 years to build a reputation
and 20 minutes to destroy it if you make the

01:31:33.639 --> 01:31:34.639
wrong choices.

01:31:34.639 --> 01:31:35.980
So I say no.

01:31:35.980 --> 01:31:40.020
The vetting process for, say, sponsors and
guests and so on is very, very...

01:31:40.020 --> 01:31:47.560
I don't want to say strict, it's not quite
the right word, but I always try to ensure

01:31:47.560 --> 01:31:56.250
that I'm following my own rules and not getting
swept away by the winds of trend or more specifically

01:31:56.250 --> 01:31:57.700
fads.

01:31:57.700 --> 01:32:04.250
This may seem like a non sequitur, and it
will be probably one of the last questions,

01:32:04.250 --> 01:32:08.960
so hopefully we can do a round two sometimes,
because I have a lot of questions that I would

01:32:08.960 --> 01:32:14.410
still love to explore, but this may seem like
an odd segue, but maybe it makes sense in

01:32:14.410 --> 01:32:15.410
some way.

01:32:15.410 --> 01:32:19.870
You were known as a goalkeeper at UBS, protecting
the balance sheet.

01:32:19.870 --> 01:32:22.610
You saved them hundreds of millions of dollars.

01:32:22.610 --> 01:32:29.920
And I'm reading here just a snippet from the
piece in The Hollywood Reporter.

01:32:29.920 --> 01:32:33.920
Your job was to analyze potential deals and
advise the bankers on whether they made sense.

01:32:33.920 --> 01:32:37.770
"In that role, he rejected 40 out of 45 potential
deals."

01:32:37.770 --> 01:32:41.750
And this is my words now, didn't make everybody
super thrilled.

01:32:41.750 --> 01:32:48.639
"However, when all 40 companies later went
bankrupt, they realized, the higher ups, that

01:32:48.639 --> 01:32:51.389
you'd saved hundreds of millions of dollars."

01:32:51.389 --> 01:32:53.060
What were you doing there?

01:32:53.060 --> 01:33:00.929
You seem to have a certain, I don't want to
say prescience, but an ability to see where

01:33:00.929 --> 01:33:04.850
the puck was going with respect to the 40
that were rejected.

01:33:04.850 --> 01:33:07.300
How did you do that?

01:33:07.300 --> 01:33:08.590
Why did you do that?

01:33:08.590 --> 01:33:11.710
Aryeh Bourkoff: First of all, I was a goalkeeper
in high school.

01:33:11.710 --> 01:33:19.550
Even as a relatively short person, I was able
to play goalie in soccer.

01:33:19.550 --> 01:33:29.040
I think that it all comes down to understanding
there'll be many more shots on goal than exists

01:33:29.040 --> 01:33:30.040
today.

01:33:30.040 --> 01:33:35.950
So don't be afraid to say no to something
today with a fear that nothing's going to

01:33:35.950 --> 01:33:37.110
come tomorrow.

01:33:37.110 --> 01:33:44.650
The sample set that you're dealing with today
is not the whole thing and play the long game.

01:33:44.650 --> 01:33:50.940
And so I felt that a lot of the companies
that we were looking at at that time were

01:33:50.940 --> 01:33:58.790
coming to us because of negative selection,
that we were getting pitched by companies

01:33:58.790 --> 01:34:01.130
that weren't necessarily the highest quality.

01:34:01.130 --> 01:34:07.869
But just because we had capital doesn't mean
that we should do it.

01:34:07.869 --> 01:34:09.330
I wanted to play quality.

01:34:09.330 --> 01:34:14.040
I wanted to only do business with the best
companies and if we didn't have the structure

01:34:14.040 --> 01:34:19.810
in place to attract those best companies today,
that's our issue to deal with.

01:34:19.810 --> 01:34:25.330
But the worst thing you could do to compound
that is make bad decisions on top of fixing

01:34:25.330 --> 01:34:27.429
your own structural issues.

01:34:27.429 --> 01:34:29.719
And that governs everything even today.

01:34:29.719 --> 01:34:34.489
When you're building the company, when you're
working with the outside world, the internal

01:34:34.489 --> 01:34:40.660
world, you're making sure that you're making
the right decisions with a quality standard

01:34:40.660 --> 01:34:49.159
for excellence at all times and that you're
not accepting mediocrity, especially if those

01:34:49.159 --> 01:34:53.040
things have a lot to do with your own fixes
that you have to make.

01:34:53.040 --> 01:34:58.610
So we spend a lot of time doing infrastructure
work internally and then planning for excellence.

01:34:58.610 --> 01:35:05.140
But if you're going to do one thing, I prefer
inactivity than mediocrity.

01:35:05.140 --> 01:35:10.680
I want to make sure that we play for excellence
and quality all the time, all the way through

01:35:10.680 --> 01:35:16.350
the company and all the way through the ripple
effects we have externally, and do fewer things

01:35:16.350 --> 01:35:20.330
with quality than scale things, obviously.

01:35:20.330 --> 01:35:26.570
Ultimately, prefer to do a lot of quality
things, but that takes a lot of structure.

01:35:26.570 --> 01:35:28.760
That's what I call scaled intimacy.

01:35:28.760 --> 01:35:33.690
If you can scale the constant of intimate
relationships and intimate deal making, that's

01:35:33.690 --> 01:35:34.690
the panacea.

01:35:34.690 --> 01:35:41.100
But otherwise, I prefer to do just a few high
quality things and then take it easy for a

01:35:41.100 --> 01:35:42.100
while.

01:35:42.100 --> 01:35:43.680
But I think that's the standard.

01:35:43.680 --> 01:35:44.680
Don't settle that.

01:35:44.680 --> 01:35:49.870
But I do have to tell you one story, though,
because you did give me some really interesting

01:35:49.870 --> 01:35:50.870
prep work.

01:35:50.870 --> 01:35:51.870
Tim Ferriss: Yes, please.

01:35:51.870 --> 01:35:56.730
Aryeh Bourkoff: Your prep work had questions
on it about certain things, "What's one of

01:35:56.730 --> 01:36:00.260
the things that you purchased for less than
a $100 that made a big impact?"

01:36:00.260 --> 01:36:03.989
And I would've said a book, so we talked about
some of these books.

01:36:03.989 --> 01:36:07.739
But you also said, "Anything particularly
interesting that you've ever had to eat or

01:36:07.739 --> 01:36:11.600
drink in your life?"

01:36:11.600 --> 01:36:17.190
I thought about it for a few minutes.

01:36:17.190 --> 01:36:21.780
I travel the world in interesting places and
you try to integrate business and life and

01:36:21.780 --> 01:36:27.440
enjoy what you're doing and appreciate the
finer things where you can.

01:36:27.440 --> 01:36:36.200
There is a drink that is served at The Hemingway
Bar at the Ritz Hotel in Paris that is called

01:36:36.200 --> 01:36:41.050
the Mach2, M-A-C-H-2.

01:36:41.050 --> 01:36:45.170
I've never had it anywhere else.

01:36:45.170 --> 01:36:51.000
It's a whiskey-based drink with chartreuse
and the essence of ginger and some ice.

01:36:51.000 --> 01:36:52.740
It's really, really good.

01:36:52.740 --> 01:36:56.620
But the funny thing about it is, as I was
thinking about it yesterday and thinking I

01:36:56.620 --> 01:37:04.690
was going to be talking to you on the podcast,
I last night went to an event in New York

01:37:04.690 --> 01:37:08.800
for the Ritz in Paris and Frame was part of
the fashion week.

01:37:08.800 --> 01:37:13.130
I was invited to because some friends were
putting it on.

01:37:13.130 --> 01:37:19.520
Unexpectedly, that bartender from the Hemingway
Bar in Paris at the Ritz was at the event.

01:37:19.520 --> 01:37:22.469
And I said, "It's unbelievable.

01:37:22.469 --> 01:37:31.360
I'm going on the Tim Ferriss podcast and he's
going to ask me about a drink and only you

01:37:31.360 --> 01:37:32.869
serve this drink in the whole world.

01:37:32.869 --> 01:37:38.800
And here you are in New York leaving your
perch at Hemingway Bar.

01:37:38.800 --> 01:37:41.520
Could you indulge me with this Mach2 drink?"

01:37:41.520 --> 01:37:43.099
He did yesterday.

01:37:43.099 --> 01:37:45.210
Tim Ferriss: That's wild.

01:37:45.210 --> 01:37:47.650
Aryeh Bourkoff: It all came together.

01:37:47.650 --> 01:37:53.540
This is how there's certain karma to my calendar
and this podcast and everything else and your

01:37:53.540 --> 01:37:55.300
questions, so I wanted to make sure that we
talked about it.

01:37:55.300 --> 01:37:57.500
Tim Ferriss: Wow, that is incredible.

01:37:57.500 --> 01:37:58.870
That's synchronicity.

01:37:58.870 --> 01:37:59.870
Now I have to try the Mach2.

01:37:59.870 --> 01:38:02.020
I look forward to that.

01:38:02.020 --> 01:38:04.240
I've needed a reason to...

01:38:04.240 --> 01:38:09.369
I've only been to Paris once really in any
meaningful way, so I need to get back.

01:38:09.369 --> 01:38:12.949
Side note, because this took place in Paris
for me, I did a lot of writing.

01:38:12.949 --> 01:38:20.700
I've done almost all of my writing for my
first three books during those witching hours.

01:38:20.700 --> 01:38:25.960
Before the morning and after the usual night,
almost all my writing was done in that I would

01:38:25.960 --> 01:38:31.139
say 10:00 p.m. to 3:00 a.m. period.

01:38:31.139 --> 01:38:33.619
Almost all of my writing in those witching
hours.

01:38:33.619 --> 01:38:36.120
Aryeh Bourkoff: Is that when there are fewer
distractions or you're clear-headed?

01:38:36.120 --> 01:38:37.410
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:38:37.410 --> 01:38:38.410
No distractions.

01:38:38.410 --> 01:38:39.410
No distractions, more energy.

01:38:39.410 --> 01:38:41.010
I tend to be like a hamster.

01:38:41.010 --> 01:38:46.520
I just have a second wind that for whatever
reason for my entire life has started very,

01:38:46.520 --> 01:38:48.580
very late.

01:38:48.580 --> 01:38:56.820
Freedom from distractions, silence, less compulsivity
to interact or text or check anything because

01:38:56.820 --> 01:38:58.610
I know the rest of the world is asleep.

01:38:58.610 --> 01:39:03.550
I shouldn't say the rest of the world, but
the rest of my network is largely asleep and

01:39:03.550 --> 01:39:12.139
it's just always been that twilight, that
creative twilight through which you can perhaps

01:39:12.139 --> 01:39:15.670
pull on threads that are normally harder to
access.

01:39:15.670 --> 01:39:18.390
I think for me that's been my experience.

01:39:18.390 --> 01:39:22.619
Well, Aryeh, I could keep going for hours.

01:39:22.619 --> 01:39:23.619
I've got plenty.

01:39:23.619 --> 01:39:32.880
So maybe another time, but is there anything
you would like to add, any recommendations

01:39:32.880 --> 01:39:36.949
or requests of the audience, things you'd
like to point their attention to?

01:39:36.949 --> 01:39:40.099
Of course people can find LionTree at liontree.com.

01:39:40.099 --> 01:39:46.040
We'll link to all of the Instagram handles
and social handles in the show notes so people

01:39:46.040 --> 01:39:47.179
will be able to access those.

01:39:47.179 --> 01:39:56.739
But are there any closing comments or requests
you would like to make before we wind to a

01:39:56.739 --> 01:39:57.739
close?

01:39:57.739 --> 01:39:59.230
Aryeh Bourkoff: It's been a great pleasure,
Tim.

01:39:59.230 --> 01:40:00.630
I could also talk to you for hours.

01:40:00.630 --> 01:40:05.430
I was looking forward to this for a long time.

01:40:05.430 --> 01:40:11.560
As we go through different periods of time
in our lives and the world at large, and we've

01:40:11.560 --> 01:40:17.940
all been through different unexpected movements
and motions and things.

01:40:17.940 --> 01:40:21.119
And that's one concept, there's going to be
more unexpected things to come.

01:40:21.119 --> 01:40:25.670
It's all about these trusted friends and communities
that we can build, which is the distinguishing

01:40:25.670 --> 01:40:31.190
feature of humans, that we can bond together
for good things and tackle the world.

01:40:31.190 --> 01:40:36.860
I think if we eliminate all the extremism,
then everyone can redefine their view of what

01:40:36.860 --> 01:40:40.150
their centers look like and then that's where
the richness lies.

01:40:40.150 --> 01:40:43.659
And whether you find it in the magic hours
in the wee hours of the night or the middle

01:40:43.659 --> 01:40:50.230
of the day, I think we can form these circles
and then really bond together, and I think

01:40:50.230 --> 01:40:51.790
the best is yet to come.

01:40:51.790 --> 01:40:53.960
I really appreciate this relationship and
what we can do together.

01:40:53.960 --> 01:40:57.449
And I really appreciate the attention of all
of your audience.

01:40:57.449 --> 01:41:03.110
And the one thing about media that is defined
is it's the distribution of content over a

01:41:03.110 --> 01:41:04.530
technology.

01:41:04.530 --> 01:41:08.710
And when you think of it that way, the technology
used to be books, newspapers, now it's podcasting.

01:41:08.710 --> 01:41:15.510
It could be different things in the future,
but the best content distributed is something

01:41:15.510 --> 01:41:22.980
that applies to everything in life that people
consume and digest, whether it's music or

01:41:22.980 --> 01:41:29.480
words of wisdom or politics, and the responsibility
is on all of us to make the best impact out

01:41:29.480 --> 01:41:34.239
of it all and I really appreciate what this
media can do for our lives.

01:41:34.239 --> 01:41:35.710
So best is yet to come.

01:41:35.710 --> 01:41:38.170
Tim Ferriss: Perfect place to close.

01:41:38.170 --> 01:41:42.400
So Aryeh, thank you so much for all your time
and for your wisdom and for your stories.

01:41:42.400 --> 01:41:47.730
I have copious notes of my own, so I'll be
following up on a lot after this conversation

01:41:47.730 --> 01:41:54.369
personally and really love what you're doing.

01:41:54.369 --> 01:42:00.590
And the more I read and learn of you directly
or indirectly, the more questions I have.

01:42:00.590 --> 01:42:05.810
So I look forward to future conversations
and for everybody listening, we'll have links

01:42:05.810 --> 01:42:12.290
to all resources, names, books, everything
related to Aryeh and LionTree in the show

01:42:12.290 --> 01:42:13.960
notes as per usual at tim.blog/podcast.

01:42:13.960 --> 01:42:22.060
And until next time, please be safe, be just
a little bit kinder than necessary and find

01:42:22.060 --> 01:42:23.060
your center.

